Text 2: Economist B argues that office demand is cyclical and will rebound as companies mandate return-to-office policies, citing recent upticks in leasing activity in several major cities.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Economist B most likely respond to Economist A's claim?$q$, $q$["By arguing that the elevated vacancy rates reflect a temporary cycle rather than a permanent shift","By agreeing that office demand has disappeared permanently","By dismissing all vacancy rate data as irrelevant to the discussion","By claiming remote work never affected office demand at all"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Economist B's position is that demand is cyclical, not permanently reduced, so B would frame A's elevated vacancy rates as part of a temporary cycle rather than a lasting shift.$q$),
  ($q$cs-24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1: Historian A contends that the fall of the trade city was primarily caused by a shift in maritime trade routes away from its harbor.

