  ($q$cs-49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than following the rigid conventions of formal calligraphy, the artist developed a style so ______ that no two of her letters were ever formed exactly the same way twice.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["idiosyncratic","standardized","illegible","traditional"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A style in which 'no two letters were ever formed exactly the same way' describes a highly individual, or idiosyncratic, approach, in contrast to rigid conventions.$q$),
  ($q$cs-50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$A history of cryptography devotes its opening section to ciphers used in ancient warfare, its middle section to the mechanical codebreaking machines of the twentieth century, and its final section to a discussion of the mathematical problems that make modern encryption difficult to break.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It traces the development of a practice chronologically from ancient methods to modern mathematical foundations.","It presents a single case study and analyzes it from multiple perspectives.","It begins with modern encryption and works backward to ancient ciphers.","It argues that ancient ciphers were more secure than modern encryption."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The three sections move in chronological order from ancient ciphers to twentieth-century machines to modern mathematics, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1: Historian A argues that lighthouse automation in the twentieth century was driven primarily by advances in electrical engineering that made unmanned lights reliable.

