  ($q$cs-61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The restorer said the antique book's binding was surprisingly ______, still holding every page firmly in place after two centuries of handling.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["durable","fragile","loose","damaged"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A binding that still holds every page firmly in place after two centuries of handling is durable, not fragile, loose, or damaged.$q$),
  ($q$ei-1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bridge was closed for repairs for nearly a year. ______ commuters were forced to take a lengthy detour through downtown.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The detour was a consequence of the bridge closure, so a cause-and-effect transition like 'As a result' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The committee reviewed dozens of proposals. ______ only two met every funding requirement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ultimately,","Additionally,","Likewise,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence states the final outcome of the review process, which 'Ultimately' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Many assumed the small bookstore would close within a year of the chain store's arrival. ______ it has since expanded to two more locations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Similarly,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bookstore expanding contradicts the expectation that it would close, so a contrast word like 'Instead' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The lecture covered the causes of the recession in great detail. ______ it left many questions about the recovery unanswered.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Therefore,","For example,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second clause presents a limitation of the lecture, contrasting with the thoroughness described first, so 'However' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The new policy reduced paperwork for small businesses. ______ it introduced a separate reporting requirement that many owners found equally burdensome.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["At the same time,","As a result,","For instance,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence describes a simultaneous downside alongside the benefit mentioned first, which 'At the same time' captures.$q$),
  ($q$ei-6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Researchers first hypothesized that the fish avoided the reef due to pollution. ______ water samples showed pollution levels well within normal range.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","Specifically,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The water sample results undercut the original hypothesis, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The festival relies entirely on volunteer labor. ______ it manages to attract over 10,000 attendees each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Attracting a large crowd despite relying only on volunteers is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The novel's first half follows a single narrator in painstaking detail. ______ the second half jumps between five different perspectives across two decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Similarly,","As a result,","In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A single narrator versus five shifting perspectives describes two structurally opposite halves, so 'By contrast' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city increased funding for public transit by 20%. ______ ridership grew by only 3% over the following year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A large funding increase producing only a small ridership gain is an unexpected, contrasting result, so 'Nonetheless' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The two researchers disagreed on nearly every methodological point. ______ they arrived at strikingly similar conclusions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Because of this,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Disagreeing on methodology but reaching similar conclusions is a contrast, best signaled by 'Yet.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The company's earnings fell short of projections for the third straight quarter. ______ its stock price rose after the announcement, surprising analysts.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","Predictably,","Similarly,","As expected,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage itself notes the outcome 'surprised analysts,' so the transition should flag the unexpected result rather than call it predictable.$q$),
  ($q$ei-12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The trail is only two miles long. ______ its steep elevation gain makes it one of the park's most difficult hikes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A short trail that is still very difficult is a contrast in expectations, which 'Nevertheless' signals correctly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Wright brothers made their first powered flight in 1903 near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina.
• The flight lasted 12 seconds and covered 120 feet.
• Their success followed years of glider experiments.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the brevity of the historic flight relative to its later significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though it lasted only 12 seconds, the Wright brothers' 1903 flight near Kitty Hawk marked the dawn of powered aviation.","The Wright brothers experimented with gliders for several years before 1903.","Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, is known for its consistent winds.","The Wright brothers' flight covered a distance of 120 feet."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A combines the flight's short duration (12 seconds) with its outsized historical significance, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Amazon River discharges more water than the next seven largest rivers combined.
• It has no bridges crossing its main stem.
• Much of its basin is inaccessible by road.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the river's exceptional water volume. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Amazon River discharges more water than the next seven largest rivers combined.","The Amazon River has no bridges crossing its main stem.","Much of the Amazon basin cannot be reached by road.","The Amazon flows through several South American countries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly conveys the river's water volume, which is the specific goal stated in the prompt.$q$),
  ($q$ei-15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Marie Curie won Nobel Prizes in both Physics (1903) and Chemistry (1911).
• She is the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences.
• She discovered the elements polonium and radium.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the uniqueness of Curie's achievement among Nobel laureates. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Marie Curie remains the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different scientific fields.","Marie Curie discovered the elements polonium and radium.","Marie Curie won her first Nobel Prize in 1903.","Nobel Prizes are awarded in several different categories."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A states the specific fact about Curie's singular distinction among laureates, matching the stated goal exactly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Barrier Reef spans over 2,300 kilometers.
• It is visible from space.
• It comprises nearly 3,000 individual reefs.$q$, $q$The student wants to convey the reef's enormous scale using a single striking detail. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Great Barrier Reef is large enough to be visible from space.","The Great Barrier Reef is home to a wide variety of marine life.","Coral reefs face threats from rising ocean temperatures.","The reef is made up of nearly 3,000 separate smaller reefs."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Being visible from space is the single most striking detail conveying scale, better fulfilling the goal than the more technical detail in choice D.$q$),
  ($q$ei-17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Esperanto was created in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof.
• It was designed to be an easy-to-learn universal second language.
• Today it has an estimated 2 million speakers worldwide.$q$, $q$The student wants to introduce Esperanto by explaining the purpose behind its creation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Esperanto was created in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof as an easy-to-learn universal second language.","Esperanto currently has an estimated 2 million speakers.","L. L. Zamenhof lived during the 19th century.","Many constructed languages have been invented over the centuries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A links the creator, date, and stated purpose (an easy-to-learn universal language), directly explaining why Esperanto was created.$q$),
  ($q$ei-18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses have three hearts.
• Two pump blood to the gills; one pumps blood to the rest of the body.
• The main heart stops beating when the octopus swims.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a surprising consequence of the octopus's unusual circulatory system. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because its main heart stops beating while swimming, an octopus tires quickly and prefers to crawl.","Octopuses have blood that appears blue due to a copper-based protein.","Octopuses can be found in oceans throughout the world.","Two of an octopus's three hearts pump blood to its gills."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A connects a specific fact from the notes (the main heart stopping during swimming) to a consequence, fulfilling the goal of explaining a surprising effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest libraries of the ancient world.
• Its exact destruction date and cause remain debated by historians.
• It is believed to have held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize scholarly uncertainty about the library's demise. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Historians still debate exactly when and how the Library of Alexandria was destroyed.","The Library of Alexandria held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.","The library was among the largest in the ancient world.","Alexandria was founded by Alexander the Great."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly addresses the ongoing scholarly debate about the library's destruction, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Bamboo can grow up to 91 centimeters in a single day.
• It is technically classified as a grass, not a tree.
• Some species are used in construction due to their strength-to-weight ratio.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight bamboo's remarkable growth rate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some bamboo species can grow as much as 91 centimeters in a single day.","Bamboo is classified as a grass rather than a tree.","Bamboo's strength-to-weight ratio makes it useful in construction.","Bamboo grows in a variety of climates around the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states a growth-rate statistic, which directly accomplishes the stated goal of highlighting bamboo's growth speed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets in the solar system.
• A day on Venus is longer than its year.
• Its surface temperature is hot enough to melt lead.$q$, $q$The student wants to introduce a fact about Venus's unusual relationship between its day length and year length. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A single day on Venus lasts longer than a full Venusian year.","Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets.","Venus's surface is hot enough to melt lead.","Venus is the second planet from the sun."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A is the only option that addresses the specific relationship between day length and year length named in the prompt.$q$),
  ($q$ei-22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Sahara Desert was once a lush, green savanna.
• This period, called the African Humid Period, ended roughly 5,000 years ago.
• Cave paintings in the region depict animals like giraffes and hippos.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific piece of evidence to support the claim that the Sahara was once green. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Cave paintings in the region depict animals such as giraffes and hippos, species associated with wetter climates.","The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world.","The African Humid Period ended about 5,000 years ago.","Deserts can form for a variety of different climatic reasons."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A offers concrete supporting evidence (cave paintings of animals tied to wetter climates) for the claim, rather than simply restating it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honey never spoils if stored properly.
• Archaeologists have found 3,000-year-old honey in Egyptian tombs that was still edible.
• Honey's low moisture content and acidity prevent bacterial growth.$q$, $q$The student wants to provide a concrete historical example that illustrates honey's exceptional shelf life. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Archaeologists have discovered 3,000-year-old honey in Egyptian tombs that remained edible.","Honey's low moisture content helps prevent bacterial growth.","Honey is produced by bees from flower nectar.","Egyptian tombs have been found to contain many preserved goods."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A gives the specific historical example (ancient Egyptian honey) requested by the prompt, rather than the general explanation in choice B.$q$),
  ($q$ei-24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Eiffel Tower grows about 15 centimeters taller in summer.
• This is due to thermal expansion of the iron.
• It was originally intended to be a temporary structure.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Eiffel Tower's height changes seasonally. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Eiffel Tower's iron structure expands in warmer temperatures, making it about 15 centimeters taller in summer.","The Eiffel Tower was originally meant to be a temporary structure.","The Eiffel Tower is a famous landmark located in Paris.","Iron is a metal that is commonly used in construction."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the cause (thermal expansion) to the effect (a 15-centimeter height change), directly explaining the phenomenon.$q$),
  ($q$ei-25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sea otters hold hands while sleeping to keep from drifting apart.
• They wrap themselves in kelp for the same reason.
• Sea otters are a keystone species in kelp forest ecosystems.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a specific behavior otters use to stay together while resting. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Sea otters often hold hands while sleeping so they don't drift apart from one another.","Sea otters are considered a keystone species in their ecosystem.","Kelp forests support a wide diversity of marine life.","Sea otters live primarily along the Pacific coast."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A directly describes the specific resting behavior (holding hands) requested by the prompt.$q$),
  ($q$ei-26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bridge was closed for repairs for six months. ______ , traffic congestion in the downtown area noticeably worsened.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The closure caused the congestion, so a result/consequence transition is needed: 'As a result.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses have three hearts.
• Two hearts pump blood to the gills.
• One heart pumps blood to the rest of the body.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify how many hearts octopuses have. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Octopuses have three hearts: two that pump blood to the gills and one that pumps blood to the rest of the body.","Octopuses are known for their unusual circulatory systems.","Blood is pumped differently in octopuses than in most animals.","Octopuses have gills that allow them to breathe underwater."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific number of hearts (three) and their functions, directly accomplishing the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The novelist rarely gives interviews. ______ whenever she does speak publicly, journalists scrutinize every word.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Nevertheless,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her rarely speaking publicly is the cause of the intense scrutiny when she does — a consequence relationship, signaled by 'Consequently.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• City A implemented a bike-share program in 2015; ridership reached 2 million trips per year by 2020.
• City B implemented a similar program in 2016; ridership reached 800,000 trips per year by 2020.
• Both cities have similar populations and climates.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight a key difference in outcomes between the two cities' programs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite similar populations and climates, City A's bike-share program achieved far higher ridership than City B's by 2020.","Both cities launched bike-share programs in the mid-2010s.","City A and City B have similar populations and climates.","City B's program began one year after City A's."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A both highlights the ridership gap (2 million vs. 800,000) and uses the similar-conditions detail to show the difference isn't explained by population or climate.$q$),
  ($q$ei-30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Proponents argue that the new tax would primarily affect large corporations. ______ , an independent analysis found that over 60% of the tax's burden would ultimately fall on consumers through higher prices.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The independent analysis contradicts the proponents' claim, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A city replaced its streetlights with LED bulbs in 2018.
• Energy costs for street lighting fell by 40% over the next two years.
• Some residents complained the new lights were too bright.$q$, $q$The student wants to acknowledge both a benefit and a drawback of the streetlight replacement. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although the LED streetlights cut energy costs by 40% within two years, some residents found them uncomfortably bright.","The city replaced its streetlights with LED bulbs in 2018.","Energy costs for street lighting fell significantly after 2018.","Some residents were unhappy with the new streetlights."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines both the benefit (lower energy costs) and the drawback (complaints about brightness) that the prompt asks for.$q$),
  ($q$ei-32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The committee reviewed dozens of proposals before selecting a finalist. ______ , the selected proposal still required several rounds of revision before approval.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Therefore,","For example,","Moreover,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite being chosen as the finalist, the proposal still needed revision — a concession relationship signaled by 'Even so.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets in the solar system.
• A day on Venus is longer than its year.
• Scientists are unsure exactly why Venus's rotation is reversed.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how unusual Venus's rotation is compared to other planets. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Unlike most planets in the solar system, Venus rotates in the opposite direction, a mystery scientists have yet to fully explain.","Venus is the second planet from the sun.","A day on Venus lasts longer than a Venusian year.","Scientists continue to study the planets in the solar system."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A directly emphasizes the unusual reversed rotation and its unexplained cause, matching the student's stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Dust and pollen gradually accumulate on solar panels over the summer. ______ their energy output can drop by more than 15% if the panels are never cleaned.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","In contrast,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in output is a direct consequence of the dust buildup described first, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries by different dynasties.
• Its total length, including all branches, exceeds 21,000 kilometers.
• Contrary to popular belief, it is not actually visible to the naked eye from the Moon.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct a common misconception about the Great Wall. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although often claimed to be visible from the Moon, the Great Wall cannot actually be seen from that distance with the naked eye.","The Great Wall was constructed over the course of many centuries.","The Great Wall's total length exceeds 21,000 kilometers, including all its branches.","Several different Chinese dynasties contributed to building the wall."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the popular misconception about visibility from the Moon, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The city commissioned dozens of murals to beautify its downtown, expecting the project to reduce vandalism. ______ reports of graffiti and property damage actually increased in the months following the murals' completion.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","As a result,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in vandalism directly contradicts the city's expectation, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The platypus is one of only five species of mammals that lay eggs.
• Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their hind legs.
• The platypus uses electroreception to detect prey underwater.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how rare it is for a mammal to lay eggs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The platypus is one of just five mammal species that lay eggs instead of giving birth to live young.","Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their hind legs.","The platypus uses electroreception to detect prey underwater.","Mammals are typically distinguished from other animals by giving birth to live young."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific rarity of egg-laying among mammals, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The archaeological team spent three seasons excavating the site without finding anything of note. ______ in the final week of the dig, they unearthed a cache of coins dating back over a thousand years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Predictably,","For instance,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Three fruitless seasons followed by a sudden discovery is a surprising turn, which 'Unexpectedly' signals; the other choices wrongly suggest the outcome was predictable or a logical consequence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Satellite data shows Antarctica lost about 40 billion tons of ice per year in the 1980s.
• By the 2010s, the annual ice loss had risen to roughly 250 billion tons per year.
• Rising ocean temperatures are believed to be accelerating the melting of ice shelves from below.$q$, $q$The student wants to illustrate how dramatically the rate of ice loss has accelerated over time. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Antarctica's annual ice loss climbed from about 40 billion tons in the 1980s to roughly 250 billion tons by the 2010s.","Rising ocean temperatures are believed to be melting ice shelves from below.","Scientists use satellites to track changes in Antarctica's ice sheet.","Antarctica contains the majority of the world's fresh water in the form of ice."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific before-and-after figures that demonstrate the scale of acceleration in ice loss.$q$),
  ($q$ei-40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bakery only opened its doors six months ago. ______ it already has a loyal following that lines up before sunrise on weekends.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Having a devoted following despite being so new is a concession, which 'Even so' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Johannes Gutenberg introduced the movable-type printing press in Europe around 1440.
• Before the press, books were copied by hand, a process that could take months.
• The printing press allowed identical copies of a text to be produced quickly and cheaply.$q$, $q$The student wants to contrast book production before and after the printing press. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Before Gutenberg's press, copying a single book by hand could take months, but afterward, identical copies could be produced quickly and cheaply.","Johannes Gutenberg introduced the movable-type printing press around 1440.","The printing press is considered one of the most important inventions in history.","Gutenberg's original printing press used movable metal type."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A is the only option that directly contrasts the slow, laborious hand-copying process with the speed and low cost enabled by the press.$q$),
  ($q$ei-42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Economists at the central bank predicted that inflation would ease significantly by the end of the year. ______ the most recent consumer price data showed inflation holding steady at nearly the same rate as six months earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The steady inflation data contradicts the bank's prediction of easing, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Arctic tern migrates from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year.
• This round trip covers roughly 70,000 kilometers.
• Arctic terns can live for more than 30 years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extraordinary distance covered by the Arctic tern's migration. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Arctic tern's annual round-trip migration between the Arctic and Antarctic covers roughly 70,000 kilometers.","Arctic terns can live for more than 30 years.","The Arctic tern breeds in the Arctic and spends its winters in the Antarctic.","Many bird species undertake long migrations each year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific 70,000-kilometer distance, which is the detail needed to emphasize the migration's extraordinary length.$q$),
  ($q$ei-44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Public health officials initially projected that a new vaccine would be available to the general public within three months of approval. ______ manufacturing delays pushed the wider rollout back by nearly a year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","As anticipated,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lengthy delay contradicts the original three-month projection, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Everest stands at 8,849 meters, the highest point above sea level on Earth.
• The mountain is officially shared by Nepal and the Tibet Autonomous Region of China.
• Climbers face extreme risks from low oxygen levels and unpredictable weather near the summit.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the specific elevation that makes Everest the world's tallest peak above sea level. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At 8,849 meters, Mount Everest is the highest point above sea level on Earth.","Mount Everest sits on the border between Nepal and the Tibet Autonomous Region of China.","Climbers attempting Everest face serious risks from low oxygen and severe weather.","Many mountaineers dream of one day climbing Mount Everest."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A gives the precise elevation figure that directly supports the claim about Everest's status as the tallest peak above sea level.$q$),
  ($q$ei-46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The region experienced its driest summer in decades. ______ the drought-tolerant garden that the Alvarez family planted last spring stayed green throughout the season.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The garden thriving despite the severe drought is a concession, which 'Even so' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Rosetta Stone contains the same decree written in three scripts: hieroglyphic, Demotic, and Greek.
• It was discovered by French soldiers in Egypt in 1799.
• Jean-François Champollion used the Greek text to help decipher Egyptian hieroglyphics in 1822.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Rosetta Stone made it possible to decode hieroglyphics. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the Rosetta Stone repeated the same decree in Greek alongside hieroglyphics, Champollion was able to use the Greek to decipher the hieroglyphic script in 1822.","The Rosetta Stone was discovered by French soldiers in Egypt in 1799.","The Rosetta Stone contains a decree written in three different scripts.","Ancient Egyptian hieroglyphics were used for thousands of years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the stone's three-script format to the specific mechanism by which Champollion deciphered hieroglyphics, fulfilling the goal of explanation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The factory's new automated assembly line was expected to eliminate most manual labor positions. ______ the company ended up hiring more workers to operate and maintain the new machinery.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Similarly,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Hiring more workers directly contradicts the expectation of eliminating jobs, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honeybees perform a "waggle dance" to communicate the location of food sources to other bees.
• The angle of the dance relative to vertical indicates the direction of the food relative to the sun.
• The duration of the waggle run indicates the distance to the food source.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain precisely how the waggle dance conveys both direction and distance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Through the waggle dance, bees signal a food source's direction by the angle of the dance relative to vertical and its distance by the duration of the waggle run.","Honeybees perform a waggle dance to communicate with other bees.","Bees use the position of the sun to help navigate.","Honeybee colonies rely on complex communication to gather food efficiently."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A specifies both mechanisms — angle for direction and duration for distance — precisely fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The violinist injured her hand in a car accident, and doctors doubted she would ever perform again. ______ she returned to the stage eighteen months later to a standing ovation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Because of this,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her triumphant return contrasts with the doctors' doubts, a relationship best signaled by 'Yet.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Titanic was, at the time, the largest passenger ship ever built.
• It sank on its maiden voyage in April 1912 after striking an iceberg.
• The ship carried far fewer lifeboats than were needed for all passengers and crew.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why so many people died when the Titanic sank. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the Titanic carried far fewer lifeboats than needed for everyone aboard, a large share of its passengers and crew had no way to escape.","The Titanic was the largest passenger ship built at the time.","The Titanic sank in April 1912 after striking an iceberg on its maiden voyage.","Shipbuilding technology advanced rapidly in the early twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A identifies the specific cause — insufficient lifeboats — that explains the high death toll, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$For decades, archaeologists maintained that the stone circle served primarily as a burial ground. ______ recent excavations of nearby postholes suggest it may have functioned as an astronomical calendar.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["More recently,","For this reason,","In the same way,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The new postholes evidence introduces a competing theory that has revised the older, long-held view, a shift that 'More recently' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coast redwoods can grow taller than 350 feet, making them the tallest trees on Earth.
• Their thick, fire-resistant bark can be over a foot thick.
• Redwoods can live for more than 2,000 years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the exceptional height of redwood trees. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Coast redwoods, which can exceed 350 feet in height, are the tallest trees on Earth.","Redwood bark can grow more than a foot thick, helping the trees survive fires.","Some individual redwood trees are more than 2,000 years old.","Redwood forests are found primarily along the coast of California and southern Oregon."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific height figure that fulfills the goal of emphasizing the redwoods' exceptional height.$q$),
  ($q$ei-54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Forecasts called for heavy rain throughout the weekend of the town fair. ______ the skies cleared just in time for the opening ceremony on Saturday morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Fortunately,","Consequently,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clearing skies are a welcome, contrasting turn against the rainy forecast, which 'Fortunately' captures.$q$),
  ($q$ei-55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• School A introduced a peer-tutoring program in 2019; its average math scores rose from 68% to 79% by 2022.
• School B did not introduce the program; its average math scores rose from 67% to 70% over the same period.
• Both schools serve similar student populations and have comparable funding levels.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to suggest that the tutoring program likely contributed to the improvement in scores. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although School A and School B serve similar populations and have comparable funding, School A's scores rose from 68% to 79% after adopting peer tutoring, far outpacing School B's gain from 67% to 70%.","School A introduced a peer-tutoring program in 2019.","School B's average math scores rose slightly between 2019 and 2022.","Both schools serve similar student populations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the comparable-conditions detail with both schools' score changes to suggest the tutoring program, rather than other factors, drove School A's larger gain.$q$),
  ($q$ei-56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The author rewrote the opening chapter of her novel eleven times before submitting the manuscript. ______ reviewers later singled out that very chapter as the strongest part of the book.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ultimately,","Similarly,","In contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The repeated revision finally paid off, an eventual outcome that 'Ultimately' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 CE, burying the Roman city of Pompeii in volcanic ash.
• The ash preserved buildings, artifacts, and even plaster casts of victims' final positions.
• Excavations of Pompeii began in the eighteenth century and continue today.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Pompeii is such a valuable source of information for historians. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because volcanic ash from the 79 CE eruption preserved Pompeii's buildings, artifacts, and even the final positions of its victims, the site offers historians an unusually detailed record of Roman life.","Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 CE, burying Pompeii in ash.","Excavations of Pompeii began in the eighteenth century and continue today.","Pompeii was a city in the Roman Empire."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains why the site is valuable — the ash's preservation of detailed evidence — directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$After three rounds of talks ended without agreement, both sides publicly stated that a deal was unlikely before the deadline. ______ a last-minute compromise on the disputed clause allowed the agreement to be signed just hours before the deadline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","For this reason,","In particular,","As predicted,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The deal being signed despite the publicly stated pessimism is a concession, which 'Nonetheless' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Emperor penguins endure Antarctic winters with temperatures as low as -60°C.
• They huddle together in large groups, rotating positions so each bird spends time in the warmer center.
• This behavior can significantly reduce individual heat loss compared to standing alone.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the specific method penguins use to conserve heat as a group. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Emperor penguins huddle in large groups and rotate positions so that every bird gets a turn in the warmer center, significantly cutting individual heat loss.","Emperor penguins can endure Antarctic winter temperatures as low as -60°C.","Emperor penguins are one of the few animals that breed during the Antarctic winter.","Antarctica is the coldest continent on Earth."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A is the only option that specifies the rotating-huddle mechanism, directly answering how penguins conserve heat as a group.$q$),
  ($q$ei-60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Light pollution from the nearby city has increased steadily over the past decade. ______ the observatory's astronomers have had to travel farther into the desert to capture clear images of faint galaxies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Having to travel farther is a direct consequence of the rising light pollution, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Thomas Edison did not invent the first electric light bulb; earlier inventors had already created working versions.
• Edison's major contribution was developing a longer-lasting, commercially practical filament.
• His bulb, using a carbonized filament, could burn for over 1,200 hours.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Edison invented the light bulb. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although Edison is widely credited with inventing the light bulb, earlier inventors had already built working versions before he improved the filament.","Edison's carbonized filament allowed his bulb to burn for over 1,200 hours.","Edison developed a longer-lasting, commercially practical filament.","Electric lighting transformed daily life in the late nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly addresses and corrects the common misconception about who invented the light bulb, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ii-1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Urban beekeeping has surged in popularity over the past decade, with hobbyists installing hives on rooftops from Brooklyn to Berlin. Advocates argue that city bees, insulated from the pesticides common in industrial agriculture, often produce healthier colonies than their rural counterparts, and that the hobby quietly bolsters local plant pollination in parks and gardens.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Urban beekeeping has grown popular and may offer ecological benefits to cities.","Rural beekeeping is less safe than urban beekeeping.","City parks depend entirely on hobbyist bees for pollination.","Pesticides have made rural farming impossible."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the rise of urban beekeeping and two proposed benefits (healthier colonies, better pollination). The other choices overstate or distort details in the passage.$q$),
  ($q$ii-2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$After decades of writing tightly plotted mysteries, novelist Elena Voss surprised critics with a sprawling, plotless meditation on memory. Some longtime fans felt betrayed by the departure; others praised it as the boldest work of her career.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe a shift in an author's writing and the mixed reaction it provoked","To argue that mystery novels are inferior to literary fiction","To criticize fans who disliked the new novel","To summarize the plot of Voss's new book"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes Voss's stylistic departure and notes both negative and positive reactions to it, matching choice A. It takes no side, so B and C are unsupported, and it never summarizes a plot.$q$),
  ($q$ii-3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$Marine biologists have long suspected that some coral species can adapt to warmer waters over successive generations.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the marine biologists' suspicion?$q$, $q$["Coral transplanted from a warm lagoon to a cooler reef showed significantly higher heat tolerance than coral native to the cooler reef.","Coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor.","Warmer ocean temperatures are linked to more frequent storms.","Many tourists visit coral reefs each year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim is about coral adapting to heat across generations. Choice A directly ties prior exposure to warmth with greater heat tolerance, supporting the adaptation hypothesis.$q$),
  ($q$ii-4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A city planner argues that adding protected bike lanes reduces the number of accidents involving cyclists.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the city planner's claim?$q$, $q$["After protected lanes were installed on Elm Street, cyclist injuries dropped 40% over two years despite a rise in the number of cyclists.","Elm Street is two miles long.","Cyclists prefer routes with fewer stoplights.","The city spent $2 million on the lanes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim concerns accident reduction. Only choice A links the bike lanes to fewer injuries, and the drop is notable precisely because it occurred even as ridership increased.$q$),
  ($q$ii-5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$A researcher claims that students who take more practice tests score higher on final exams. In a study of 200 students, those who completed five or more practice tests averaged 88 on the final exam, while those who completed fewer than five averaged 74.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the study that supports the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["Students completing five or more practice tests averaged 14 points higher than those who completed fewer.","All 200 students improved their scores over the semester.","The study included students from ten different schools.","Each practice test took about 30 minutes to complete."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim links practice-test frequency to higher scores. Choice A directly reports the 14-point gap between the two groups, which is the evidence supporting the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$A nutritionist claims a new diet leads to greater weight loss than a standard low-fat diet. Over 12 weeks, participants on the new diet lost an average of 9 pounds, compared with 5 pounds for those on the standard low-fat diet.$q$, $q$Which choice most directly supports the nutritionist's claim?$q$, $q$["Participants on the new diet lost an average of 4 more pounds than those on the standard diet.","The study lasted 12 weeks.","Some participants dropped out of the study before it ended.","The new diet restricts carbohydrate intake."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim is comparative: the new diet caused greater weight loss. Only choice A states the actual difference in pounds lost between the two groups.$q$),
  ($q$ii-7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The museum's newest exhibit displays tools recovered from a 3,000-year-old settlement, including finely carved fishhooks and woven nets far more intricate than researchers expected from a community previously believed to rely solely on hunting.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The settlement's economy likely depended on fishing to a greater extent than previously assumed.","The settlement had no contact with hunting communities.","The fishhooks discovered were purely decorative and never used.","Researchers had always known the community fished extensively."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The discovery of intricate fishing tools 'far more' developed than expected, in a community 'believed to rely solely on hunting,' implies fishing played a bigger economic role than previously thought.$q$),
  ($q$ii-8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A software company's internal survey found that employees who worked remotely at least three days a week reported higher job satisfaction, yet the company's voluntary turnover rate stayed roughly the same after expanding remote options.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["Job satisfaction and turnover rate are not necessarily driven by the same factors at this company.","Remote work directly caused the turnover rate to increase.","Every employee at the company prefers remote work.","The company plans to eliminate remote work options."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Satisfaction rose but turnover held steady, suggesting the two outcomes are not tightly linked at this company — exactly what choice A concludes without overreaching.$q$),
  ($q$ii-9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Obsidian, a volcanic glass prized by ancient toolmakers for its razor-sharp edges, can be chemically 'fingerprinted' to its volcanic source. By matching obsidian tools found at distant archaeological sites to specific quarries, researchers have traced trade routes spanning hundreds of miles, revealing exchange networks far more extensive than early excavators imagined.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Chemical analysis of obsidian tools has revealed unexpectedly far-reaching ancient trade networks.","Obsidian is the sharpest natural material known to researchers.","Early excavators correctly predicted the extent of ancient trade.","Volcanic glass can only be found near active quarries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage's point is that sourcing obsidian chemically has shown trade networks were more extensive than once believed — the opposite of choice C, and beyond the narrower details in B and D.$q$),
  ($q$ii-10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian argues that a 19th-century famine was worsened by government policy rather than by crop failure alone.$q$, $q$Which choice would most directly support the historian's argument?$q$, $q$["Grain exports from the affected region continued at high volumes even as local food prices soared.","The crop failure was caused by a fungal blight.","The famine lasted for several consecutive years.","Neighboring regions also experienced poor harvests that year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Continuing grain exports during a famine points to a policy choice worsening food scarcity, directly supporting the claim that policy — not just crop failure — deepened the crisis.$q$),
  ($q$ii-11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Despite operating in one of the coldest inhabited regions on Earth, the research station relies almost entirely on solar power for six months of the year, switching to wind and stored batteries only during the darkest winter months.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The region experiences significant seasonal variation in daylight.","Solar power does not function in cold climates.","Wind power is the station's primary energy source year-round.","The station has no access to batteries during summer."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The shift from solar to wind/batteries specifically in the 'darkest winter months' implies a seasonal light cycle extreme enough to make solar power impractical part of the year.$q$),
  ($q$ii-12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$For nearly a century, the landscapes of painter Clara Whitfield hung largely unseen in private collections, dismissed by early critics as derivative. A recent retrospective, drawing on newly uncovered letters, recasts her muted palettes and unconventional framing as deliberate innovations rather than technical shortcomings.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["A reevaluation of Whitfield's work suggests her once-dismissed techniques were intentional artistic choices.","Whitfield's paintings were destroyed in a fire and are now lost.","Critics have always admired Whitfield's use of color and framing.","Whitfield primarily painted portraits rather than landscapes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The retrospective reframes techniques once seen as flaws as deliberate choices — the reversal choice A captures. Choice C contradicts the passage's mention of early dismissal.$q$),
  ($q$ii-13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$An economist argues that a minimum-wage increase in City X did not significantly reduce employment. In the two years following the increase, City X's unemployment rate changed from 4.2% to 4.3%, while neighboring cities without the increase saw rates rise from 4.0% to 4.6%.$q$, $q$Which finding from the data most directly supports the economist's argument?$q$, $q$["City X's unemployment rate rose less than that of neighboring cities without the wage increase.","City X's unemployment rate increased at all during the period.","Neighboring cities are larger in population than City X.","The wage increase in City X took effect immediately upon passage."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The relevant comparison is City X's small rise (0.1 point) against a larger rise elsewhere (0.6 points) without the policy — implying the wage increase did not drive up unemployment relative to the trend.$q$),
  ($q$ii-14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$The novel's narrator repeatedly insists that she remembers every detail of that summer with perfect clarity, yet her account of the events shifts each time she retells it to a different character.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The narrator's memory of the events may be less reliable than she claims.","The narrator is deliberately lying to the reader.","Every character in the novel doubts the narrator's account.","The events the narrator describes never actually occurred."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A gap between a claim of 'perfect clarity' and a shifting retelling suggests unreliable memory, without requiring the stronger, unsupported claims in B, C, or D.$q$),
  ($q$ii-15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Contrary to the assumption that deep-sea environments are biologically barren, expeditions to hydrothermal vents have found dense communities of tube worms, crabs, and bacteria thriving without sunlight, sustained instead by chemical energy from the vents themselves.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Life can flourish in the deep sea through chemical rather than solar energy sources.","Hydrothermal vents are extremely dangerous to all marine life.","Sunlight is required for every deep-sea ecosystem to survive.","Tube worms are the only species found near hydrothermal vents."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage's point is a correction of assumptions: deep-sea vent life thrives on chemical, not solar, energy — precisely what choice A states.$q$),
  ($q$ii-16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A teacher argues that incorporating short daily writing exercises improves students' essay scores more than occasional long assignments do.$q$, $q$Which finding would most directly support the teacher's argument?$q$, $q$["Classes that wrote for 10 minutes daily improved average essay scores by 15%, while classes assigned one long essay per month showed no significant change.","Writing is considered an important skill across many careers.","Some students report disliking daily writing exercises.","The school recently adopted a new writing curriculum."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim compares two specific approaches to writing practice; only choice A directly measures and compares outcomes for both groups.$q$),
  ($q$ii-17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The company's founder has declined every interview request for over a decade, yet the firm's annual shareholder letter — written personally each year — reveals detailed opinions on everything from monetary policy to competitor strategy.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The founder avoids public appearances but is still willing to communicate views in writing.","The founder has no opinions on monetary policy.","The company has stopped publishing its shareholder letters.","The founder is unaware of competitors' strategies."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Declining interviews while personally writing detailed, opinionated letters each year implies a preference for written over spoken public communication, not a general aversion to sharing views.$q$),
  ($q$ii-18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Octopuses solve mazes, open jars, and even appear to play, all despite having a nervous system organized radically differently from that of vertebrates — most of their neurons reside in their arms rather than in a central brain. Researchers increasingly view this decentralized intelligence not as a lesser version of vertebrate cognition but as an entirely distinct evolutionary solution to complex problem-solving.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Octopus intelligence, though structured differently from vertebrate intelligence, represents its own sophisticated evolutionary approach to problem-solving.","Octopuses are considered less intelligent than most vertebrate species.","All of an octopus's neurons are located in its central brain.","Mazes are the only reliable way to test octopus intelligence."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage explicitly reframes octopus cognition as 'an entirely distinct evolutionary solution,' not an inferior one — matching choice A and contradicting choice B and C.$q$),
  ($q$ii-19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$A public health official argues that a new vaccination campaign significantly reduced flu-related hospitalizations. Hospitalizations fell from 1,200 cases the prior flu season to 430 cases the season following the campaign, even as the region's population grew slightly.$q$, $q$Which finding most directly supports the official's argument?$q$, $q$["Flu-related hospitalizations dropped by more than half the season after the campaign began, despite a growing population.","The vaccination campaign began in October.","Flu season typically lasts several months.","Some patients experienced mild vaccine side effects."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sharp drop in hospitalizations — occurring even as population grew, which would tend to raise case counts — is the direct evidence supporting the campaign's effectiveness.$q$),
  ($q$ii-20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The city's new zoning ordinance permits accessory dwelling units on any residential lot, yet in its first year only a handful of homeowners applied for permits, most of them in the same three affluent neighborhoods.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["Factors beyond legal permission, such as cost, may be limiting broader adoption of accessory dwelling units.","No homeowners in the city are interested in accessory dwelling units.","The zoning ordinance was overturned within its first year.","Homeowners in affluent neighborhoods are barred from applying for permits."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Legal permission alone didn't produce widespread uptake, and adoption clustered in wealthier areas — suggesting a nonlegal barrier, such as construction cost, is limiting broader participation.$q$),
  ($q$ii-21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Translators of ancient epic poetry face a persistent dilemma: hew closely to the original's literal wording and risk producing stilted, unreadable verse, or prioritize the spirit and rhythm of the piece and risk drifting from its precise meaning. Recent translators have increasingly opted for the latter, arguing that a poem unread is a poem lost regardless of its fidelity.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Many modern translators favor readability and rhythm over strict literal accuracy when rendering ancient poetry.","All translators agree that literal accuracy matters most.","Ancient epic poetry cannot be translated into modern languages.","Rhythm is irrelevant to the translation of poetry."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage states recent translators 'increasingly opted for' prioritizing spirit and rhythm over literal fidelity — the trend described in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$ii-22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A biologist argues that a species of frog has recently expanded its range northward due to warming temperatures.$q$, $q$Which finding would most directly support the biologist's argument?$q$, $q$["The frog has been documented breeding in ponds 200 miles north of its historical range over the past decade, coinciding with warmer average winters.","The frog species is brightly colored.","Frogs are classified as amphibians.","The species was first scientifically described in the 1950s."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A links a documented range expansion to warming temperatures, directly supporting the climate-driven range-shift claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The startup's founders publicly credit 'relentless customer feedback' for every major product decision, though internal emails obtained by a reporter show the flagship feature was greenlit after a single conversation with an investor, months before any customer had used the product.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The founders' public account of how decisions are made may not fully reflect the company's actual process.","Customer feedback played no role in any company decision.","The investor personally built the flagship feature.","Internal emails at the company are always made public."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A gap between the public narrative (customer-driven decisions) and the internal record (an investor-driven decision predating customer use) implies the public account is at least incomplete.$q$),
  ($q$ii-24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Long dismissed as a niche hobby, competitive chess has seen a surge in youth participation since online platforms made it possible to play thousands of opponents without leaving home. Coaches report that the accessibility, more than any single champion's fame, has driven the growth.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Online accessibility, rather than celebrity influence, is largely responsible for chess's growing popularity among young players.","Chess has always been an extremely popular hobby among young people.","Coaches strongly disagree about why chess participation is growing.","Online platforms have made chess a less competitive game."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Coaches specifically credit accessibility 'more than any single champion's fame,' directly supporting choice A's claim about the cause of chess's growth.$q$),
  ($q$ii-25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$An agricultural scientist argues that drip irrigation conserves significantly more water than traditional flood irrigation without reducing crop yield. Fields using drip irrigation used 35% less water per acre while yielding within 2% of the output from flood-irrigated fields.$q$, $q$Which finding most directly supports the scientist's argument?$q$, $q$["Drip-irrigated fields used substantially less water while producing nearly identical yields to flood-irrigated fields.","Flood irrigation is one of the oldest known irrigation techniques.","Drip irrigation systems require plastic tubing to install.","The study examined fields located in a single region."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim has two parts — water savings and preserved yield — and only choice A reports data addressing both, matching the study's actual figures.$q$),
  ($q$ii-26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$A city planner proposed replacing several downtown parking lots with a public park, arguing that green space would attract more visitors than parking ever did. Local business owners initially opposed the plan, fearing lost customers, but many later reported increased foot traffic once the park opened.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["A park built in place of parking lots ended up benefiting nearby businesses despite initial opposition.","Business owners were correct that removing parking lots would hurt sales.","City planners rarely listen to the concerns of local business owners.","Public parks are always more popular than parking lots."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes initial opposition followed by increased foot traffic once the park opened — a benefit to businesses despite their early concerns.$q$),
  ($q$ii-27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$The museum's new wing was designed with movable walls so that exhibits could be rearranged without construction crews. Curators say this flexibility lets them respond quickly to loaned collections that arrive on short notice.$q$, $q$It can most reasonably be inferred from the text that$q$, $q$["the museum can adapt its exhibit space quickly when new collections become available.","the museum's new wing cost more than any previous renovation.","curators prefer permanent exhibits over rotating ones.","construction crews are frequently on site at the museum."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The movable walls let curators respond quickly to short-notice loans without construction, directly supporting the inference that the museum can adapt quickly.$q$),
  ($q$ii-28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A novelist claims that her hometown's harsh winters shaped her writing style, citing how she once wrote, 'I learned to say more with less, the way a bare tree says everything about winter without a single leaf.'$q$, $q$Which quotation from the novelist best supports the claim that harsh winters shaped her writing style?$q$, $q$["'I learned to say more with less, the way a bare tree says everything about winter without a single leaf.'","'I have always loved reading mystery novels.'","'My publisher suggested I write a sequel.'","'Winters where I live now are much milder.'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This quotation directly links a winter image (a bare tree) to her spare writing style, supporting the claim that winter shaped her style.$q$),
  ($q$ii-29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Public libraries have begun lending more than books: seed libraries let patrons borrow packets of vegetable seeds to plant at home, then return seeds harvested from their own gardens at the end of the season.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe how libraries have expanded their lending programs to include garden seeds.","To argue that libraries should stop lending books.","To explain why vegetable gardening has declined in popularity.","To compare public libraries in different cities."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text's sole focus is describing the seed-library lending program, matching choice A; none of the other choices reflect its content.$q$),
  ($q$ii-30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$A nutritionist claims that a new granola bar contains meaningfully less added sugar than leading competitors. Lab tests found the new bar contains 4 grams of added sugar per serving, compared to an average of 11 grams among the five best-selling competitor bars.$q$, $q$Which finding most directly supports the nutritionist's claim?$q$, $q$["The new granola bar contains less than half the added sugar of the average competitor bar.","The new granola bar is sold in fewer stores than its competitors.","The five competitor bars have been on the market longer.","The new granola bar contains more fiber than some competitors."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim is specifically about reduced added sugar, and 4 grams versus an average of 11 grams is less than half — direct quantitative support.$q$),
  ($q$ii-31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Researchers tracking a coral reef noticed that sections shaded by floating mats of algae recovered more quickly from a bleaching event than unshaded sections, even though water temperatures were identical across the reef.$q$, $q$The information in the text most strongly suggests that$q$, $q$["shade from algae mats may help coral recover from bleaching independent of water temperature.","water temperature has no effect on coral bleaching.","algae mats always damage coral reefs.","unshaded coral sections never recover from bleaching."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since shaded sections recovered faster despite identical temperatures, shade itself — not temperature — appears linked to faster recovery.$q$),
  ($q$ii-32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Two studies of urban tree cover reached different emphases: one concluded that tree canopy primarily reduces summer temperatures, while the other concluded that canopy loss primarily increases stormwater runoff. Taken together, the studies suggest that urban trees serve multiple, largely independent environmental functions, and losing them creates compounding problems rather than a single, isolated one.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Considered together, the two studies indicate that urban trees provide several distinct benefits whose loss creates multiple simultaneous problems.","The two studies directly contradict each other and cannot both be correct.","Reducing summer temperatures is more important than managing stormwater runoff.","Urban tree cover has no measurable effect on stormwater runoff."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text explicitly states the studies, 'taken together,' show trees serve 'multiple, largely independent' functions whose loss 'compounds' — matching choice A, not a contradiction.$q$),
  ($q$ii-33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian argues that a 19th-century trade agreement was less influential than commonly believed, presenting a diary entry from a merchant of the era: 'The new treaty was signed with great ceremony, yet our trade routes and prices continued exactly as before, as though the ink had dried on paper no one troubled to read.'$q$, $q$Which quotation from the merchant's diary best supports the historian's argument?$q$, $q$["'yet our trade routes and prices continued exactly as before, as though the ink had dried on paper no one troubled to read.'","'The new treaty was signed with great ceremony.'","'I have kept this diary since I was young.'","'Merchants travel widely in search of goods.'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The historian argues the treaty had little real effect; this quotation directly shows trade continued unchanged, supporting that argument.$q$),
  ($q$ii-34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Engineers studying the aqueducts that once supplied ancient Rome have found that many stretches descend at a gradient of less than one inch per hundred feet, a level of precision achieved without modern surveying instruments. The find has led historians to credit Roman engineers with a far more sophisticated grasp of hydraulics than earlier scholars assumed.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Roman engineers achieved a level of hydraulic precision in aqueduct design that surprises modern historians.","Modern surveying instruments are more accurate than those used by the Romans.","Roman aqueducts were built primarily to transport goods rather than water.","Historians have always recognized the sophistication of Roman hydraulics."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage states the precise gradient finding led historians to credit Romans with more sophisticated hydraulics than 'earlier scholars assumed,' matching choice A; choice D contradicts this reversal.$q$),
  ($q$ii-35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$An architect argues that a new type of insulation panel, made from recycled denim fibers, retains heat more effectively than traditional fiberglass insulation, citing a building manager's log entry: 'Since we replaced the fiberglass with the denim panels last winter, our heating bills have dropped nearly a third, even during the coldest weeks in January.'$q$, $q$Which quotation from the building manager's log best supports the architect's claim?$q$, $q$["'Since we replaced the fiberglass with the denim panels last winter, our heating bills have dropped nearly a third, even during the coldest weeks in January.'","'Denim insulation panels are made from recycled fabric scraps.'","'We plan to install the same panels in a second building next year.'","'Fiberglass insulation has been used in construction for decades.'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only this quotation reports an actual outcome — reduced heating bills after the switch — that supports the claim of superior heat retention.$q$),
  ($q$ii-36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$A sleep researcher claims that students who sleep at least eight hours the night before an exam perform better than those who sleep less. In a study of 150 students, those who slept eight or more hours averaged 85 on the exam, while those who slept less than six hours averaged 71.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the study that supports the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["Students who slept eight or more hours averaged 14 points higher on the exam than those who slept less than six hours.","The study included 150 students total.","The exam covered material from an entire semester.","Some students reported feeling anxious before the exam."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim links sleep duration to exam performance, and only choice A reports the actual point gap between the two sleep groups.$q$),
  ($q$ii-37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Ornithologists tracking a songbird species that migrates at night have found that birds passing over brightly lit cities now arrive at their breeding grounds, on average, four days later than birds that follow darker rural flight paths, even though the two routes are nearly identical in distance.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["Artificial light along migration routes may disrupt or delay birds' nighttime navigation.","Birds that migrate over cities travel a much longer distance than those on rural routes.","Nighttime migration has been abandoned by this songbird species.","Rural flight paths are more dangerous for migrating birds than urban ones."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since the routes are nearly identical in distance, the delay associated with the lit route points to the light itself as a likely disruptive factor, not the distance traveled.$q$),
  ($q$ii-38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Contemporary composer Hana Ito has become known for compositions that include extended passages of near-total silence, sometimes lasting a full minute within an otherwise conventional orchestral piece. Rather than treating silence as an absence of music, Ito describes it as a compositional tool as deliberate and expressive as any note she writes.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Composer Hana Ito treats silence as an intentional, expressive element of her music rather than a mere gap.","Ito's compositions contain no traditional orchestral instruments.","Silence in music is generally considered a technical flaw by composers.","Ito's use of silence has made her music unpopular with orchestras."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage directly states Ito 'describes silence as a compositional tool as deliberate and expressive as any note,' matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$ii-39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$An astronomer argues that a distant exoplanet likely has a thick atmosphere capable of retaining heat, citing an analysis note from the observing team: 'Temperature readings on the planet's night side remain nearly as high as on its sunlit side, a pattern we would not expect unless heat were being trapped and redistributed by a substantial atmosphere.'$q$, $q$Which quotation from the analysis note best supports the astronomer's argument?$q$, $q$["'Temperature readings on the planet's night side remain nearly as high as on its sunlit side, a pattern we would not expect unless heat were being trapped and redistributed by a substantial atmosphere.'","'The exoplanet orbits its star once every nine days.'","'This system was first observed by a space telescope launched a decade ago.'","'The planet is roughly the same size as Neptune.'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only this quotation ties an observed pattern — even night/day temperatures — to the presence of a heat-trapping atmosphere, directly supporting the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$A city council member claims that a door-to-door outreach campaign increased household participation in the recycling program. Before the campaign, 42% of households in the pilot neighborhood recycled regularly; six months after the campaign, that figure rose to 68%.$q$, $q$Which finding most directly supports the council member's claim?$q$, $q$["Household recycling participation in the pilot neighborhood rose from 42% to 68% after the campaign.","The pilot neighborhood contains about 900 households.","The outreach campaign used flyers printed on recycled paper.","The city has run similar campaigns in the past."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim is about increased participation, and only choice A reports the actual before-and-after participation rates.$q$),
  ($q$ii-41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Divers exploring a shipwreck off the coast of Sicily recovered amphorae bearing pottery marks from workshops in North Africa, Spain, and the eastern Mediterranean, all packed together in the same cargo hold.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The ship was likely involved in trade connecting multiple distant regions rather than a single route.","The amphorae were manufactured entirely in Sicily.","The ship sank before it was ever loaded with cargo.","North Africa and Spain had no trade contact with the eastern Mediterranean."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cargo from three distant regions found together suggests a ship engaged in multi-region trade, not a route confined to one origin point.$q$),
  ($q$ii-42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Director Marcus Ade has spent the last five years staging plays by 17th-century women playwrights whose work vanished from theater repertoires after their deaths. Audiences, he says, are often surprised to learn these comedies and tragedies predate more famous works long credited with originating certain now-common stage techniques.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe an effort to bring overlooked historical plays back to the stage and highlight their overlooked influence.","To argue that modern plays are inferior to those written in the 17th century.","To criticize audiences for being unfamiliar with theater history.","To summarize the plot of a specific 17th-century comedy."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes Ade's revival project and the surprising historical influence of these plays, matching choice A; it does not compare eras' quality or summarize a specific plot.$q$),
  ($q$ii-43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$An economist argues that expanded broadband access has slowed population decline in small rural towns, citing a resident survey response: 'I moved my consulting business back home last year because I could finally get a reliable connection — before that, staying here for work simply wasn't an option.'$q$, $q$Which quotation from the survey best supports the economist's argument?$q$, $q$["'I moved my consulting business back home last year because I could finally get a reliable connection — before that, staying here for work simply wasn't an option.'","'I grew up in this town and still visit for the holidays.'","'The town recently repaved its main street.'","'Broadband service became available in most of the county last year.'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This quotation directly connects new broadband access to a resident's decision to remain in town for work, supporting the claim that access slowed population decline.$q$),
  ($q$ii-44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$A materials engineer claims that a new anti-reflective coating increases solar panel energy output without added cost. Panels with the coating produced an average of 6.8% more electricity over one year than uncoated panels of the same model, while manufacturing costs per panel increased by less than 1%.$q$, $q$Which finding from the study most directly supports the engineer's claim?$q$, $q$["Coated panels produced 6.8% more electricity annually while costing less than 1% more to manufacture.","The study compared panels installed in a desert climate.","Solar panels of this model have been sold since 2015.","The coating was developed over three years of laboratory testing."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim has two parts — higher output and negligible added cost — and only choice A reports data addressing both figures from the study.$q$),
  ($q$ii-45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$After a chef replaced his restaurant's fixed year-round menu with one that changes weekly based on available local produce, reservation requests rose sharply, and many diners began returning multiple times within a single season to try new dishes.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The rotating, produce-based menu likely gave diners a reason to visit the restaurant more than once per season.","Diners preferred the restaurant's food before the menu changed.","The chef stopped using local produce entirely after the change.","Reservation requests declined after the new menu was introduced."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Diners returning multiple times to try 'new dishes' implies the changing menu itself, not just the food quality, drove repeat visits.$q$),
  ($q$ii-46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$For thirty years, linguist Dara Feld has recorded elderly speakers of a language with fewer than twenty remaining fluent speakers, compiling not just vocabulary but the specific idioms, jokes, and songs that dictionaries alone would never preserve. She argues that a language's grammar can survive in academic papers, but its humor and music die with its last speakers unless someone listens in time.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Feld's decades of recording work aim to preserve aspects of a dying language, like humor and song, that formal documentation alone would miss.","Dictionaries are sufficient to preserve every aspect of an endangered language.","The language Feld studies has already gone completely extinct.","Feld primarily focuses on translating academic grammar papers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage's point is that Feld's recordings capture idioms, jokes, and songs that 'dictionaries alone would never preserve' — directly matching choice A and contradicting choice B.$q$),
  ($q$ii-47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian argues that a 19th-century expedition's slow progress across a mountain range resulted from unusually severe terrain rather than poor planning, pointing to the expedition leader's journal: 'We advanced barely two miles today, cutting steps into ice with hand axes the entire way, our supplies far heavier than any trail we had crossed before.'$q$, $q$Which quotation from the journal best supports the historian's argument?$q$, $q$["'We advanced barely two miles today, cutting steps into ice with hand axes the entire way, our supplies far heavier than any trail we had crossed before.'","'We departed from the coastal town on the fourth of March.'","'Several members of the team had crossed mountains before.'","'Our maps were purchased from a reputable cartographer.'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This quotation describes physically severe conditions directly slowing progress, supporting the claim that terrain, not planning, caused the delay.$q$),
  ($q$ii-48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$A transportation official claims that a new congestion pricing toll reduced downtown traffic without significantly hurting local businesses. In the six months after the toll began, average downtown commute times fell by 18%, while total sales reported by downtown businesses fell by less than 1%.$q$, $q$Which finding most directly supports the official's claim?$q$, $q$["Commute times fell by 18% while downtown business sales dropped by less than 1%.","The toll costs drivers $4 during peak hours.","Downtown contains over 200 small businesses.","The toll program was modeled on one used in another city."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim has two parts — reduced traffic and minimal harm to business — and only choice A provides data on both.$q$),
  ($q$ii-49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A trackway of fossilized footprints, preserved in what was once soft mud, shows dozens of same-sized dinosaur prints moving in the same direction at a steady pace, with no evidence of prints crossing, doubling back, or scattering.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["These dinosaurs may have traveled together as a coordinated group rather than individually.","The dinosaurs that made these tracks were of many different species.","The trackway shows evidence of a predator attack.","The footprints were made by dinosaurs moving in random directions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Same-sized prints moving together, at a steady pace, in one direction without scattering suggests coordinated group movement rather than individual, random travel.$q$),
  ($q$ii-50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Photographer Iris Kwan is known for images that require exposures lasting several hours, during which passing clouds, moving water, and shifting light blur into soft, dreamlike streaks. Kwan insists the resulting images are not distortions of reality but reveal a passage of time that the human eye, limited to an instant, can never directly perceive.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Kwan's long-exposure technique captures a dimension of time that ordinary human vision cannot perceive.","Kwan's photographs are widely considered inaccurate representations of her subjects.","Long exposure photography requires exposures lasting only a few seconds.","The human eye can naturally perceive the passage of time in the way Kwan's camera does."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage states Kwan's images reveal 'a passage of time' invisible to 'the human eye, limited to an instant,' directly matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$ii-51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A business journalist argues that a company's new four-day workweek policy improved employee morale beyond what leadership expected, citing an internal memo from the CEO: 'I anticipated some improvement in morale, but I did not expect nearly every department head to report their teams asking to keep the schedule permanently, even those who were skeptical before we began the trial.'$q$, $q$Which quotation from the CEO's memo best supports the journalist's argument?$q$, $q$["'I anticipated some improvement in morale, but I did not expect nearly every department head to report their teams asking to keep the schedule permanently, even those who were skeptical before we began the trial.'","'We began the four-day workweek trial at the start of the fiscal year.'","'The company employs about 600 people across three offices.'","'Other companies have experimented with similar schedules.'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This quotation explicitly contrasts the CEO's expectations with the stronger-than-expected positive response, directly supporting the claim of morale improvement beyond expectations.$q$),
  ($q$ii-52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$An agronomist claims that a slow-release fertilizer increases crop yield while reducing nutrient runoff into nearby waterways. Fields treated with the slow-release fertilizer yielded 12% more corn per acre than fields using standard fertilizer, while nitrogen runoff measured in nearby streams dropped by 30%.$q$, $q$Which finding most directly supports the agronomist's claim?$q$, $q$["Slow-release fertilizer fields yielded 12% more corn while nearby nitrogen runoff fell 30%.","The study was conducted over a single growing season.","Standard fertilizer has been used by farmers for many decades.","The slow-release fertilizer costs more per bag than standard fertilizer."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim requires both higher yield and reduced runoff, and only choice A reports data on both outcomes measured in the study.$q$),
  ($q$ii-53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$Chemists analyzing a painting attributed to a 17th-century master detected traces of a synthetic pigment that was not invented until the 20th century.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The painting was likely not created by the artist to whom it has been attributed.","The 17th-century master invented synthetic pigments ahead of his time.","All 17th-century paintings contain modern synthetic pigments.","The chemists were unable to determine anything about the painting's pigments."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A pigment that did not exist until centuries after the claimed creation date implies the painting cannot genuinely date to the 17th century.$q$),
  ($q$ii-54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$A city's bike-share program initially concentrated its docking stations in wealthier downtown districts. After residents in outlying neighborhoods petitioned for equal access, the city added dozens of new stations across the city, and ridership among lower-income residents rose substantially within a year.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Expanding bike-share stations beyond wealthy areas led to a substantial rise in ridership among lower-income residents.","The bike-share program was canceled due to low ridership.","Wealthier neighborhoods no longer have any bike-share stations.","Residents in outlying neighborhoods opposed the bike-share expansion."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage describes the petition-driven expansion and its result — rising ridership among lower-income residents — matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$ii-55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A biographer argues that a poet's years living alone on a remote coastline directly shaped the spare, isolated imagery of her later collections, citing a letter the poet wrote to a friend: 'Out here, with no one to talk to but gulls and the tide, my lines have gotten shorter, as if the ocean edited out every unnecessary word.'$q$, $q$Which quotation from the poet's letter best supports the biographer's argument?$q$, $q$["'Out here, with no one to talk to but gulls and the tide, my lines have gotten shorter, as if the ocean edited out every unnecessary word.'","'I have always enjoyed reading poetry by the fire in winter.'","'My friend wrote back within the month.'","'The coastline here is known for its rocky cliffs.'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This quotation directly connects the poet's isolated coastal life to a change in her writing style, supporting the biographer's argument.$q$),
  ($q$ii-56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$A school district claims that a new small-group reading intervention improved literacy scores specifically among struggling readers rather than students overall. Among students who began below grade level, average reading scores rose 22 points after the intervention, while students who began at or above grade level saw scores rise by only 3 points on average.$q$, $q$Which finding most directly supports the district's claim that the intervention specifically helped struggling readers?$q$, $q$["Below-grade-level students gained 22 points on average, compared with a 3-point gain for at-or-above-grade-level students.","The intervention program ran for one full school year.","The district serves students across twelve elementary schools.","Teachers received training before implementing the program."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim is about a difference in benefit between two groups, and only choice A reports the gap in score gains between struggling and non-struggling readers.$q$),
  ($q$ii-57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Chemical analysis of pottery fragments found at an inland settlement revealed clay compositions matching coastal deposits over 150 miles away, despite the settlement having local clay sources readily available.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The settlement likely obtained the pottery through trade or exchange with coastal communities rather than producing it locally.","The settlement had no local clay sources of any kind.","Coastal communities never traveled inland.","The pottery fragments were all manufactured at the inland settlement."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since local clay was available yet the pottery matches distant coastal deposits, the pottery most plausibly reached the settlement through trade rather than local production.$q$),
  ($q$ii-58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Rather than extending a centralized power grid across difficult mountain terrain, engineers have begun installing small, independent solar-and-battery microgrids in remote villages. Each microgrid serves only a few dozen households but can be installed in weeks rather than the years often required to build long-distance transmission lines.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Small, localized microgrids offer a faster alternative to extending centralized power grids into remote areas.","Centralized power grids are always faster to build than microgrids.","Remote mountain villages have rejected all forms of electrification.","Microgrids can supply electricity to entire large cities."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage contrasts slow, centralized grid extension with quickly installed microgrids serving a few dozen households — matching choice A's emphasis on speed and scale.$q$),
  ($q$ii-59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A science historian argues that an overlooked 19th-century engineer, not the inventor typically credited, first designed the mechanism later used in modern refrigeration, citing a letter the engineer wrote to a colleague: 'I have built a working model that cools a sealed chamber using compressed gas, and I am certain the principle could one day replace ice entirely for food storage.'$q$, $q$Which quotation from the engineer's letter best supports the historian's argument?$q$, $q$["'I have built a working model that cools a sealed chamber using compressed gas, and I am certain the principle could one day replace ice entirely for food storage.'","'I corresponded with this colleague for many years.'","'Ice has long been used to preserve food in warmer months.'","'My workshop is located near the harbor.'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This quotation describes a working refrigeration mechanism the engineer built, directly supporting the claim that he designed the underlying principle first.$q$),
  ($q$ii-60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$A workplace wellness researcher claims that a brief daily meditation program reduces employee stress levels. Employees who completed a 10-minute guided meditation each workday for eight weeks reported a 27% drop in self-assessed stress scores, while a comparison group with no program reported only a 4% drop over the same period.$q$, $q$Which finding most directly supports the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["The meditation group's stress scores fell 27%, compared with a 4% drop in the comparison group over the same period.","The meditation sessions were guided by a recorded audio program.","The study included employees from several different departments.","Employees were allowed to choose the time of day for their session."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim is comparative, and only choice A reports the actual difference in stress-score reduction between the meditation group and the comparison group.$q$),
  ($q$ii-61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers who have recorded the same population of humpback whales for over thirty years found that the population's songs change gradually year to year, yet whales in a neighboring, rarely-contacted population continue to sing a version of the song nearly identical to recordings from three decades ago.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["Contact between whale populations may play a role in how quickly their songs change over time.","Humpback whale songs never change within a single population.","The neighboring population has stopped singing entirely.","All whale populations sing identical songs regardless of contact with other groups."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The population with frequent contact/study shows gradual change while the more isolated population's song stayed nearly static, suggesting contact between groups relates to how songs evolve over time.$q$)
