insert into public.questions
  (id, module, domain, skill, difficulty, passage, prompt, choices, answer, explanation)
values
  ($q$rw-1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Marine biologist Ayana Elizabeth Johnson argues that solutions to climate change are not ______; the ocean, she notes, offers a wealth of untapped strategies, from restoring coastal wetlands to expanding offshore wind.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["scarce","abundant","theoretical","expensive"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause after the semicolon says the ocean offers 'a wealth of untapped strategies.' For the passage to be logical, the first clause must claim solutions are NOT rare — so 'scarce' fits the 'not ___' structure.$q$),
  ($q$rw-2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence$q$, 2, $q$A student claims that urban community gardens meaningfully reduce household grocery spending for participating families.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["Families in a community-garden program reported spending 18% less on produce over a growing season than comparable non-participating families.","Community gardens are most often located in densely populated neighborhoods.","Participants said gardening improved their mood and sense of community.","The number of community gardens in the city doubled over five years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim is specifically about reduced grocery spending. Only choice A provides quantitative evidence tying garden participation to lower spending on food.$q$),
  ($q$rw-3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$By the time the expedition reached the summit ______ nearly every member had run out of supplemental oxygen.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ",": ","; ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory dependent clause ('By the time...summit') must be separated from the main clause with a comma. A colon or semicolon would incorrectly imply the first part can stand alone.$q$),
  ($q$rw-4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The researchers noted that the migratory patterns of the songbirds ______ significantly over the past decade, likely in response to warming temperatures.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have shifted","has shifted","shifting","to shift"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject 'patterns' is plural, so it needs the plural verb 'have shifted.' 'Over the past decade' signals present-perfect tense.$q$),
  ($q$rw-5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Solar panel efficiency has improved dramatically in the last fifteen years. ______ the cost of installation has fallen by more than 70%, making solar accessible to far more households.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,","Nevertheless,","In contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds a second positive development (falling cost) to the first (rising efficiency). 'Moreover' signals addition of a supporting point.$q$),
  ($q$rw-6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas$q$, 3, $q$Historians once portrayed the medieval period as a cultural 'dark age,' but recent scholarship emphasizes vibrant networks of trade, translation, and intellectual exchange that spanned continents. Manuscripts preserved and expanded classical knowledge, while universities emerged as centers of rigorous debate.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Newer scholarship challenges the view of the medieval period as intellectually stagnant.","Medieval universities were the first institutions to preserve classical texts.","Trade networks were the most important feature of medieval life.","Historians have always agreed about the vibrancy of medieval culture."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage contrasts the old 'dark age' view with recent scholarship showing intellectual vibrancy. Choice A captures that reframing; the others overstate details or contradict the text.$q$),
  ($q$rw-7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though critics initially dismissed the composer's work as ______, later generations recognized its intricate structure and emotional depth, elevating pieces once ignored into staples of the concert repertoire.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["slight","melodic","controversial","revolutionary"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The contrast ('Though...but later recognized...depth') requires an initially negative, dismissive judgment. 'Slight' means trivial or insubstantial, fitting the dismissal that later proved wrong.$q$),
  ($q$rw-8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The city council approved the new bike lane ______ construction is scheduled to begin in the spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," "," and, "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses ('The council approved...' and 'construction is scheduled...') must be joined by a semicolon (or a comma + conjunction). Only the semicolon is offered correctly.$q$),
  ($q$m-1$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3x + 7 = 22, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","7","15","29/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3x + 7 = 22 → 3x = 15 → x = 5.$q$),
  ($q$m-2$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios & proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A recipe requires 2 cups of flour for every 3 cups of sugar. If a baker uses 8 cups of flour, how many cups of sugar are needed?$q$, $q$["12","10","9","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Set up the proportion 2/3 = 8/x. Cross-multiply: 2x = 24, so x = 12 cups of sugar.$q$),
  ($q$m-3$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A jacket originally priced at $80 is discounted by 25%. A sales tax of 8% is then applied to the discounted price. What is the final price?$q$, $q$["$64.80","$60.00","$66.00","$62.40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Discounted price: 80 × 0.75 = $60. With tax: 60 × 1.08 = $64.80.$q$),
  ($q$m-4$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 2x + y = 11 and x − y = 1, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4","3","5","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Add the equations: (2x + y) + (x − y) = 11 + 1 → 3x = 12 → x = 4.$q$),
  ($q$m-5$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 2, null, $q$The expression x² − 5x + 6 can be factored as (x − a)(x − b). What is the value of a + b?$q$, $q$["5","6","1","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 2)(x − 3), so a and b are 2 and 3. Their sum is 5 (which equals the coefficient of the middle term with sign flipped).$q$),
  ($q$m-6$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line passes through the points (1, 4) and (3, 10). What is the slope of the line?$q$, $q$["3","2","6","1/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (10 − 4) / (3 − 1) = 6 / 2 = 3.$q$),
  ($q$m-7$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 2, null, $q$If 2^(x+1) = 32, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4","5","3","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$32 = 2^5, so x + 1 = 5, giving x = 4.$q$),
  ($q$m-8$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Geometry$q$, 2, null, $q$A right triangle has legs of length 6 and 8. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["10","14","48","√28"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$By the Pythagorean theorem: √(6² + 8²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10.$q$),
  ($q$m-9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$The list of numbers 4, 8, 8, 10, 15 has what median?$q$, $q$["8","9","10","8.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With 5 values sorted, the median is the middle (3rd) value: 8.$q$),
  ($q$m-10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 4x + 3. For what value of x does f reach its minimum?$q$, $q$["2","−2","4","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The vertex x-coordinate is −b/(2a) = −(−4)/(2·1) = 2. The parabola opens upward, so the minimum occurs at x = 2.$q$),
  ($q$m-11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 3, null, $q$A car rental costs $40 per day plus $0.20 per mile. If the total charge for a one-day rental was $70, how many miles were driven?$q$, $q$["150","175","125","350"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$70 = 40 + 0.20m → 30 = 0.20m → m = 150 miles.$q$),
  ($q$m-12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle has a circumference of 12π. What is its area?$q$, $q$["36π","12π","6π","144π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Circumference = 2πr = 12π → r = 6. Area = πr² = π(6²) = 36π.$q$),
  ($q$adv-1$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x² − 7x + 12 = 0, what is the product of the solutions?$q$, $q$["12","7","−12","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² − 7x + 12 = (x − 3)(x − 4), so the roots are 3 and 4, and their product is 12.$q$),
  ($q$adv-2$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 2x² + 3x − 2 = 0, what is the sum of the solutions?$q$, $q$["−3/2","3/2","2","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2x² + 3x − 2 = (2x − 1)(x + 2) = 0, so x = 1/2 or x = −2. Their sum is 1/2 + (−2) = −3/2.$q$),
  ($q$adv-3$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$The function f(x) = (x − 3)² + 5 has what minimum value?$q$, $q$["5","3","−5","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In vertex form (x − h)² + k, the minimum value is k = 5, occurring at x = 3.$q$),
  ($q$adv-4$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A population is modeled by P(t) = 500(1.08)^t, where t is measured in years. What is the population, to the nearest whole number, after 3 years?$q$, $q$["630","500","580","700"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$500(1.08)^3 = 500(1.259712) ≈ 630.$q$),
  ($q$adv-5$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 1, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (3x²)(4x³)?$q$, $q$["12x^5","7x^6","12x^6","7x^5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiply coefficients and add exponents: 3·4 = 12, and x²·x³ = x^5, giving 12x^5.$q$),
  ($q$adv-6$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x² − 16 = 0, what is the positive value of x?$q$, $q$["4","−4","16","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² − 16 = (x − 4)(x + 4) = 0, so x = 4 or x = −4. The positive value is 4.$q$),
  ($q$adv-7$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$If y = x² and y = 2x + 3, what is the sum of all values of x that satisfy both equations?$q$, $q$["2","3","−1","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² = 2x + 3 → x² − 2x − 3 = 0 → (x − 3)(x + 1) = 0, so x = 3 or x = −1. Their sum is 2.$q$),
  ($q$adv-8$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 2, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (x³)⁴ / x⁵?$q$, $q$["x^7","x^12","x^17","x^2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(x³)⁴ = x^12, and x^12 / x^5 = x^(12−5) = x^7.$q$),
  ($q$adv-9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$If x² + 8x + 7 = 0, what is the sum of the solutions?$q$, $q$["−8","−1","−7","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Completing the square: (x + 4)² = 9 → x + 4 = ±3 → x = −1 or x = −7. Their sum is −8.$q$),
  ($q$adv-10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The polynomial f(x) = x³ − 2x² + kx − 6 has x = 2 as one of its roots. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["3","6","−3","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(2) = 8 − 8 + 2k − 6 = 0 → 2k − 6 = 0 → k = 3.$q$),
  ($q$adv-11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 2, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to x^(1/2) · x^(3/2)?$q$, $q$["x^2","x^(3/4)","x^(2/3)","x"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Add the exponents: 1/2 + 3/2 = 2, so the expression simplifies to x².$q$),
  ($q$adv-12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The height of a tossed ball is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 64t + 5, where t is in seconds. At what time does the ball reach its maximum height?$q$, $q$["2","4","1","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The vertex occurs at t = −b/(2a) = −64/(2 · −16) = 2 seconds.$q$),
  ($q$adv-13$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$If f(x) = x² and g(x) = f(x − 2) + 3, what is the value of g(0)?$q$, $q$["7","4","1","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$g(0) = f(0 − 2) + 3 = f(−2) + 3 = (−2)² + 3 = 4 + 3 = 7.$q$),
  ($q$adv-14$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 2, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (x + 3)(x − 3) + 9?$q$, $q$["x^2","x^2 − 9","x^2 + 9","x^2 + 6x"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(x + 3)(x − 3) = x² − 9, and adding 9 gives x² − 9 + 9 = x².$q$),
  ($q$adv-15$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A radioactive sample of 80 grams halves in mass every 5 years. How many grams remain after 15 years?$q$, $q$["10","20","40","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$15 years is 3 half-lives: 80 · (1/2)³ = 80/8 = 10 grams.$q$),
  ($q$adv-16$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 3, null, $q$Which value of x satisfies the inequality x² − 5x + 6 < 0?$q$, $q$["2.5","1","4","3.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 2)(x − 3) < 0 when 2 < x < 3. Only 2.5 falls in that interval.$q$),
  ($q$adv-17$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 2, null, $q$If f(x) = 2x + 1 and g(x) = x², what is the value of f(g(3))?$q$, $q$["19","7","10","37"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$g(3) = 9, so f(g(3)) = f(9) = 2(9) + 1 = 19.$q$),
  ($q$adv-18$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$For what positive value of k does the equation x² + kx + 9 = 0 have exactly one real solution?$q$, $q$["6","9","3","36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$One real solution requires the discriminant to equal 0: k² − 4(1)(9) = 0 → k² = 36 → k = 6 (taking the positive value).$q$),
  ($q$adv-19$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 1, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to √50?$q$, $q$["5√2","10√5","2√5","25√2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$√50 = √(25 · 2) = 5√2.$q$),
  ($q$adv-20$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Which function represents exponential decay?$q$, $q$["y = 100(0.75)^x","y = 100(1.25)^x","y = 100 + 0.75x","y = 0.75x^100"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Exponential decay has the form y = a(b)^x with 0 < b < 1. Only y = 100(0.75)^x satisfies this.$q$),
  ($q$adv-21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$For the function f(x) = −2x² + 3x − 1, what happens to f(x) as x approaches infinity?$q$, $q$["f(x) approaches negative infinity","f(x) approaches positive infinity","f(x) approaches 0","f(x) approaches 1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since the leading coefficient (−2) is negative and the degree is even, the graph opens downward, so f(x) → −∞ as x → ∞.$q$),
  ($q$adv-22$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$If y = x² − 4 and y = −x + 2, what is the greatest value of x that satisfies both equations?$q$, $q$["2","−3","4","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² − 4 = −x + 2 → x² + x − 6 = 0 → (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0, so x = −3 or x = 2. The greatest value is 2.$q$),
  ($q$adv-23$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function y = x² − 6x + 5 has what minimum value?$q$, $q$["−4","5","−9","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Completing the square: y = (x − 3)² − 9 + 5 = (x − 3)² − 4, so the minimum value is −4.$q$),
  ($q$adv-24$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 3^(2x) = 81, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["2","4","1.5","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$81 = 3^4, so 2x = 4 → x = 2.$q$),
  ($q$adv-25$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 1, null, $q$If f(x) = x³ − 3x, what is the value of f(−1)?$q$, $q$["2","−2","4","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(−1) = (−1)³ − 3(−1) = −1 + 3 = 2.$q$),
  ($q$adv-26$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$If (x − 3)² = 25, what is the positive value of x?$q$, $q$["8","−2","5","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Taking the square root of both sides: x − 3 = ±5, so x = 8 or x = −2. The positive value is 8.$q$),
  ($q$adv-27$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$What is the sum of the y-coordinates of the solutions to the system y = x² and y = 2x + 3?$q$, $q$["10","4","13","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Setting x² = 2x + 3 gives x² − 2x − 3 = 0 → (x − 3)(x + 1) = 0, so x = 3 or x = −1. The corresponding y-values are 9 and 1, which sum to 10.$q$),
  ($q$adv-28$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The population of a town is modeled by P(t) = 4000(1.05)^t, where t is the number of years after 2010. According to this model, what was the population in 2012?$q$, $q$["4,410","4,200","4,000","4,800"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$At t = 2: P(2) = 4000(1.05)² = 4000(1.1025) = 4,410.$q$),
  ($q$adv-29$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$If f(x) = 2x + 1 and g(x) = x² − 3, what is the value of f(g(2))?$q$, $q$["3","5","1","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$g(2) = 2² − 3 = 1. Then f(1) = 2(1) + 1 = 3.$q$),
  ($q$adv-30$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 2, null, $q$For what values of x is x² − 5x + 6 < 0?$q$, $q$["2 < x < 3","x < 2 or x > 3","−3 < x < −2","x < −2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factoring: (x − 2)(x − 3) < 0. Since the parabola opens upward, the expression is negative between its roots, so 2 < x < 3.$q$),
  ($q$adv-31$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (x² − 9) / (x − 3) for x ≠ 3?$q$, $q$["x + 3","x − 3","x + 9","x − 9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² − 9 factors as (x − 3)(x + 3), so (x² − 9)/(x − 3) simplifies to x + 3 for x ≠ 3.$q$),
  ($q$adv-32$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the value of (2⁵ · 2³) / 2⁴?$q$, $q$["16","8","32","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using exponent rules: 2^(5+3−4) = 2⁴ = 16.$q$),
  ($q$adv-33$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of y = −2(x − 1)² + 8 is a parabola. What is the maximum value of y?$q$, $q$["8","1","−2","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The equation is in vertex form with vertex (1, 8). Since the leading coefficient (−2) is negative, the parabola opens downward, so 8 is the maximum value of y.$q$),
  ($q$adv-34$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 1, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (5x⁴)(2x²)?$q$, $q$["10x^6","7x^6","10x^8","7x^8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiply coefficients and add exponents: 5·2 = 10, and x⁴·x² = x^6, giving 10x^6.$q$),
  ($q$adv-35$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2^x = 32, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","4","16","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$32 = 2^5, so x = 5.$q$),
  ($q$adv-36$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 1, null, $q$The value of an investment is modeled by V(t) = 2000(1.04)^t, where t is measured in years. What is the value of the investment after 2 years, to the nearest dollar?$q$, $q$["$2,163","$2,080","$2,000","$2,240"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$V(2) = 2000(1.04)² = 2000(1.0816) = 2,163.2, which rounds to $2,163.$q$),
  ($q$adv-37$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the value of 3⁻² · 3⁴?$q$, $q$["9","3","81","1/9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Add the exponents: 3^(−2+4) = 3² = 9.$q$),
  ($q$adv-38$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 1, null, $q$If f(x) = x + 4 and g(x) = 3x, what is the value of g(f(1))?$q$, $q$["15","7","13","19"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(1) = 1 + 4 = 5, so g(f(1)) = g(5) = 3(5) = 15.$q$),
  ($q$adv-39$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$The function f(x) = (x + 2)² − 7 has what minimum value?$q$, $q$["−7","2","7","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In vertex form (x − h)² + k, the minimum value is k = −7, occurring at x = −2.$q$),
  ($q$adv-40$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If y = x² and y = 9, what is the sum of all values of x that satisfy both equations?$q$, $q$["0","9","6","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² = 9 gives x = 3 or x = −3, which sum to 0.$q$),
  ($q$adv-41$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 1, null, $q$If f(x) = 2x³ − x + 5, what is the value of f(1)?$q$, $q$["6","5","4","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(1) = 2(1)³ − 1 + 5 = 2 − 1 + 5 = 6.$q$),
  ($q$adv-42$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x² − 9x + 20 = 0, what is the sum of the solutions?$q$, $q$["9","20","−9","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² − 9x + 20 = (x − 4)(x − 5), so the roots are 4 and 5, and their sum is 9.$q$),
  ($q$adv-43$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 1, null, $q$Which value of x satisfies the inequality x² − 9 < 0?$q$, $q$["0","4","−4","3.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3) < 0 when −3 < x < 3. Only 0 falls in that interval.$q$),
  ($q$adv-44$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 2, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (2x − 5)(x + 4)?$q$, $q$["2x² + 3x − 20","2x² − 3x − 20","2x² + 3x + 20","2x² − 20x + 3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(2x − 5)(x + 4) = 2x² + 8x − 5x − 20 = 2x² + 3x − 20.$q$),
  ($q$adv-45$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (x² − 4x − 21) / (x − 7) for x ≠ 7?$q$, $q$["x + 3","x − 3","x + 7","x − 7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² − 4x − 21 factors as (x − 7)(x + 3), so the expression simplifies to x + 3 for x ≠ 7.$q$),
  ($q$adv-46$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 5^(x+1) = 125, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["2","3","4","1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$125 = 5³, so x + 1 = 3 → x = 2.$q$),
  ($q$adv-47$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 3, null, $q$If 4^(2x−1) = 8^(x+1), what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","1","−5","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write both sides with base 2: 4^(2x−1) = 2^(4x−2) and 8^(x+1) = 2^(3x+3). Setting exponents equal: 4x − 2 = 3x + 3 → x = 5.$q$),
  ($q$adv-48$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A car depreciates in value according to V(t) = 24000(0.85)^t, where t is measured in years. To the nearest hundred dollars, what is the car's value after 2 years?$q$, $q$["$17,300","$20,400","$16,000","$18,000"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$V(2) = 24000(0.85)² = 24000(0.7225) = 17,340, which rounds to $17,300.$q$),
  ($q$adv-49$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of bacteria in a culture doubles every 4 hours. If there are 300 bacteria at time t = 0 (in hours), which function models the number of bacteria, B(t), after t hours?$q$, $q$["B(t) = 300 · 2^(t/4)","B(t) = 300 · 2^(4t)","B(t) = 300 · 4^(t/2)","B(t) = 300 · 2^t"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Doubling every 4 hours means the exponent is t/4: B(t) = 300 · 2^(t/4). Check: B(4) = 300 · 2¹ = 600, double the original amount.$q$),
  ($q$adv-50$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the value of (4³ · 4⁻¹) / 4⁰?$q$, $q$["16","4","64","1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4⁰ = 1, and 4³ · 4⁻¹ = 4^(3−1) = 4² = 16, so the quotient is 16/1 = 16.$q$),
  ($q$adv-51$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 3, null, $q$If x > 0 and x^(2/3) = 9, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["27","6","13.5","81"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Raise both sides to the power 3/2: x = 9^(3/2) = (√9)³ = 3³ = 27.$q$),
  ($q$adv-52$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 2, null, $q$If f(x) = x² − 2 and g(x) = 3x + 1, what is the value of f(g(1))?$q$, $q$["14","10","7","35"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$g(1) = 3(1) + 1 = 4, so f(g(1)) = f(4) = 4² − 2 = 14.$q$),
  ($q$adv-53$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$If f(x) = 2x − 3 and g(x) = x² + 1, what is the value of g(f(−1))?$q$, $q$["26","−9","24","11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(−1) = 2(−1) − 3 = −5, so g(f(−1)) = g(−5) = (−5)² + 1 = 26.$q$),
  ($q$adv-54$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f(x) = 3(x − 2)² + 4 is graphed in the xy-plane. What are the coordinates of the vertex?$q$, $q$["(2, 4)","(−2, 4)","(2, −4)","(4, 2)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In vertex form a(x − h)² + k, the vertex is (h, k) = (2, 4).$q$),
  ($q$adv-55$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(x) = −x² + 6x − 5 reaches its maximum value at x = a. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["3","−3","5","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For f(x) = −x² + 6x − 5, the maximum occurs at x = −b/(2a) = −6/(2(−1)) = 3.$q$),
  ($q$adv-56$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If y = x² − 2x and y = x, what is the greatest value of x that satisfies both equations?$q$, $q$["3","0","2","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x² − 2x = x → x² − 3x = 0 → x(x − 3) = 0, so x = 0 or x = 3. The greatest value is 3.$q$),
  ($q$adv-57$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The polynomial f(x) = x³ + 2x² − 5x − 6 has x = −1 as one of its roots. What is the product of the other two roots?$q$, $q$["−6","6","−3","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Dividing f(x) by (x + 1) gives x² + x − 6 = (x + 3)(x − 2), so the other roots are −3 and 2, and their product is −6.$q$),
  ($q$adv-58$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 3x² − 12 = 0, what is the positive value of x?$q$, $q$["2","4","−2","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3x² − 12 = 0 → x² = 4 → x = ±2. The positive value is 2.$q$),
  ($q$adv-59$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 3, null, $q$For what values of x is the inequality 2x² − x − 6 ≥ 0 true?$q$, $q$["x ≤ −3/2 or x ≥ 2","−3/2 ≤ x ≤ 2","x ≤ −2 or x ≥ 3/2","x < −3/2 or x > 2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factoring: 2x² − x − 6 = (2x + 3)(x − 2). Since the parabola opens upward, the expression is nonnegative outside the roots, so x ≤ −3/2 or x ≥ 2.$q$),
  ($q$alg-1$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 4x − 9 = 23, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["8","6","3.5","32"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4x − 9 = 23 → 4x = 32 → x = 8.$q$),
  ($q$alg-2$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2(x + 3) = 5x − 6, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4","2","6","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2x + 6 = 5x − 6 → 12 = 3x → x = 4.$q$),
  ($q$alg-3$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line passes through the points (2, 5) and (4, 11). What is the y-intercept of the line?$q$, $q$["−1","1","5","11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (11 − 5)/(4 − 2) = 3. Using y = 3x + b with (2, 5): 5 = 3(2) + b → b = −1.$q$),
  ($q$alg-4$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + y = 10 and x − y = 2, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["4","6","8","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Subtract the equations: (x + y) − (x − y) = 10 − 2 → 2y = 8 → y = 4.$q$),
  ($q$alg-5$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest integer value of x that satisfies 3x − 5 > 10?$q$, $q$["6","5","4","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3x − 5 > 10 → 3x > 15 → x > 5, so the smallest integer satisfying this is 6.$q$),
  ($q$alg-6$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A gym membership costs $25 to join plus $15 per month. If a member has paid a total of $205, how many months has the membership been active?$q$, $q$["12","10","13","180"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$25 + 15m = 205 → 15m = 180 → m = 12.$q$),
  ($q$alg-7$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If y = 2x − 1 and 3x + y = 14, what is the value of x + y?$q$, $q$["8","3","5","11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute: 3x + (2x − 1) = 14 → 5x = 15 → x = 3, so y = 2(3) − 1 = 5. Thus x + y = 8.$q$),
  ($q$alg-8$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear, with f(0) = 4 and f(2) = 10. What is the value of f(5)?$q$, $q$["19","14","10","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (10 − 4)/(2 − 0) = 3, so f(x) = 3x + 4. Then f(5) = 3(5) + 4 = 19.$q$),
  ($q$alg-9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line given by y = 2x + 3 is parallel to a second line that passes through (0, −1). What is the slope of the second line?$q$, $q$["2","−1","3","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Parallel lines have equal slopes, so the second line also has slope 2.$q$),
  ($q$alg-10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If |x − 4| = 9, what is the sum of all possible values of x?$q$, $q$["8","9","−5","13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x − 4 = 9 gives x = 13; x − 4 = −9 gives x = −5. The sum of the two solutions is 13 + (−5) = 8.$q$),
  ($q$alg-11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 3, null, $q$How many solutions does the system y = 2x + 5 and y = 2x + 3 have?$q$, $q$["0","1","2","Infinitely many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Setting the equations equal: 2x + 5 = 2x + 3 → 5 = 3, which is false. The lines are parallel with different intercepts, so there is no solution.$q$),
  ($q$alg-12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A parking garage charges $5 for the first hour and $2 for each additional hour. What is the maximum number of whole hours a driver can park without exceeding $19?$q$, $q$["8","7","9","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$5 + 2(h − 1) ≤ 19 → 2(h − 1) ≤ 14 → h − 1 ≤ 7 → h ≤ 8, so the maximum is 8 hours.$q$),
  ($q$alg-13$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line given by 3x + 2y = 12?$q$, $q$["−3/2","3/2","2/3","−2/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving for y: 2y = −3x + 12 → y = (−3/2)x + 6, so the slope is −3/2.$q$),
  ($q$alg-14$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the x-intercept of the line 2x − 5y = 20?$q$, $q$["10","−4","4","−10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Set y = 0: 2x = 20 → x = 10.$q$),
  ($q$alg-15$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3x − y = 7 and x + y = 9, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["5","4","9","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: 4x = 16 → x = 4. Then from x + y = 9, y = 5.$q$),
  ($q$alg-16$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A cell phone plan costs $20 plus $0.10 per minute of calls. If a customer's bill was $35, how many minutes of calls were made?$q$, $q$["150","15","175","350"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$20 + 0.10m = 35 → 0.10m = 15 → m = 150.$q$),
  ($q$alg-17$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$Company A charges $50 plus $3 per unit produced. Company B charges $20 plus $5 per unit produced. For what number of units is the total cost the same for both companies?$q$, $q$["15","10","30","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$50 + 3x = 20 + 5x → 30 = 2x → x = 15.$q$),
  ($q$alg-18$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the greatest integer value of x that satisfies −3 < 2x + 1 ≤ 7?$q$, $q$["3","2","−2","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−4 < 2x ≤ 6 → −2 < x ≤ 3, so the greatest integer value is 3.$q$),
  ($q$alg-19$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 3, null, $q$The formula A = P(1 + rt) can be solved for r in terms of A, P, and t. Which expression is equivalent to r?$q$, $q$["(A − P)/(Pt)","(A − Pt)/P","A/(Pt) − 1","(A − P)/t"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A = P + Prt → A − P = Prt → r = (A − P)/(Pt).$q$),
  ($q$alg-20$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$Sarah is 4 years older than twice Tom's age. If Sarah is 22 years old, how old is Tom?$q$, $q$["9","11","18","13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$22 = 2t + 4 → 18 = 2t → t = 9.$q$),
  ($q$alg-21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A water tank had 80 gallons at time t = 0 and 20 gallons at t = 6 minutes, draining at a constant rate. What is the drain rate in gallons per minute?$q$, $q$["10","60","6","600"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The tank lost 60 gallons over 6 minutes: 60/6 = 10 gallons per minute.$q$),
  ($q$alg-22$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 3, null, $q$How many solutions does the system 6x − 2y = 10 and y = 3x − 5 have?$q$, $q$["Infinitely many","0","Exactly 1","Exactly 2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting: 6x − 2(3x − 5) = 10 → 6x − 6x + 10 = 10 → 10 = 10, which is always true, so the system has infinitely many solutions.$q$),
  ($q$alg-23$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which ordered pair is a solution to the inequality y ≤ 3x − 2?$q$, $q$["(2, 3)","(0, 0)","(−1, 2)","(1, 5)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For (2, 3): 3 ≤ 3(2) − 2 = 4, which is true. The other points fail the inequality when checked.$q$),
  ($q$alg-24$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line has a slope of −3 and passes through (1, 2). What is the value of y when x = 4?$q$, $q$["−7","−10","7","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$y − 2 = −3(x − 1) → y = −3x + 5. At x = 4: y = −12 + 5 = −7.$q$),
  ($q$alg-25$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$Adult movie tickets cost $12 and child tickets cost $8. A group bought 5 tickets for a total of $52. How many adult tickets were purchased?$q$, $q$["3","2","4","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let a + c = 5 and 12a + 8c = 52. Substituting c = 5 − a: 12a + 8(5 − a) = 52 → 4a = 12 → a = 3.$q$),
  ($q$alg-26$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 3, null, $q$If 3x − 2y = 12 and x + y = 9, what is the value of xy?$q$, $q$["18","27","15","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$From x + y = 9, y = 9 − x. Substituting: 3x − 2(9 − x) = 12 → 5x = 30 → x = 6, so y = 3. Then xy = 18.$q$),
  ($q$alg-27$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 3, null, $q$The sum of two numbers is 42. The larger number is 2 more than 3 times the smaller number. What is the larger number?$q$, $q$["32","10","36","28"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let the smaller number be x, so the larger is 3x + 2. Then x + (3x + 2) = 42 → 4x = 40 → x = 10, and the larger number is 3(10) + 2 = 32.$q$),
  ($q$alg-28$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$A phone plan charges a flat monthly fee plus a constant rate per minute of calls. A 40-minute call costs $14, and a 70-minute call costs $20. What is the flat monthly fee?$q$, $q$["$6","$8","$4","$10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rate per minute is (20 − 14)/(70 − 40) = 6/30 = $0.20. Using the 40-minute call: 14 = fee + 0.20(40) = fee + 8, so the fee is $6.$q$),
  ($q$alg-29$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If 2x + 3y ≤ 18 and x = 3, what is the maximum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["4","6","3","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 3: 6 + 3y ≤ 18 → 3y ≤ 12 → y ≤ 4, so the maximum value of y is 4.$q$),
  ($q$alg-30$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 3, null, $q$For what value of k does the system of equations 2x + 3y = 6 and 4x + ky = 10 have no solution?$q$, $q$["6","5","8","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A system has no solution when the lines are parallel but distinct: the coefficient ratios must match while the constant ratio differs. Setting 2/4 = 3/k gives k = 6; since 6/10 ≠ 2/4, the lines are parallel and distinct.$q$),
  ($q$alg-31$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation 5x − 2y = 20 is graphed in the xy-plane. What is the x-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["4","10","−4","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The x-intercept occurs where y = 0: 5x = 20 → x = 4.$q$),
  ($q$alg-32$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the largest integer value of x that satisfies −4x + 9 > −11?$q$, $q$["4","5","3","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−4x + 9 > −11 → −4x > −20 → x < 5 (the inequality flips when dividing by a negative number). The largest integer less than 5 is 4.$q$),
  ($q$alg-33$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$Line ℓ has an x-intercept of 4 and a y-intercept of −8. What is the slope of line ℓ?$q$, $q$["2","−2","1/2","−1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The line passes through (4, 0) and (0, −8). Slope = (0 − (−8)) / (4 − 0) = 8/4 = 2.$q$),
  ($q$alg-34$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 5x + 7 = 32, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","3","7","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$5x + 7 = 32 → 5x = 25 → x = 5.$q$),
  ($q$alg-35$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation for converting Celsius to Fahrenheit is F = (9/5)C + 32. Which expression gives C in terms of F?$q$, $q$["(5/9)(F − 32)","(9/5)(F − 32)","(5/9)F − 32","(5/9)F + 32"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$F − 32 = (9/5)C → C = (5/9)(F − 32).$q$),
  ($q$alg-36$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function g is linear. The table shows g(1) = 7 and g(4) = 19. What is g(10)?$q$, $q$["43","40","46","37"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (19 − 7)/(4 − 1) = 4, so g(x) = 4x + 3. Then g(10) = 4(10) + 3 = 43.$q$),
  ($q$alg-37$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2x + y = 15 and y = x − 3, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["6","3","9","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute y = x − 3 into 2x + y = 15: 2x + (x − 3) = 15 → 3x = 18 → x = 6.$q$),
  ($q$alg-38$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A vendor sells popcorn bags for $4 each after paying a $30 booth fee. What is the minimum number of bags the vendor must sell to make a profit greater than $50?$q$, $q$["21","20","19","30"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4n − 30 > 50 → 4n > 80 → n > 20, so the minimum whole number of bags is 21.$q$),
  ($q$alg-39$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 3, null, $q$Which of the following ordered pairs is NOT a solution to the inequality 4x − 3y > 12?$q$, $q$["(0, 0)","(5, 2)","(6, 0)","(10, 5)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Testing (0, 0): 4(0) − 3(0) = 0, which is not greater than 12, so (0, 0) is not a solution. The other points each satisfy the inequality: for example, 4(5) − 3(2) = 14 > 12.$q$),
  ($q$alg-40$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A taxi charges a $3 flat fee plus $2.50 per mile. If a ride costs $15.50, how many miles was the ride?$q$, $q$["5","6","4","12.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3 + 2.5m = 15.5 → 2.5m = 12.5 → m = 5.$q$),
  ($q$alg-41$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If (2/3)(x − 6) = 10, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["21","9","6","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(2/3)(x − 6) = 10 → x − 6 = 15 → x = 21.$q$),
  ($q$alg-42$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 3, null, $q$The equation for the perimeter of a rectangle is P = 2l + 2w. Which expression gives w in terms of P and l?$q$, $q$["(P − 2l)/2","(P − l)/2","P − 2l","(P + 2l)/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P = 2l + 2w → 2w = P − 2l → w = (P − 2l)/2.$q$),
  ($q$alg-43$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line has a slope of 4 and passes through the point (0, −3). What is the equation of the line?$q$, $q$["y = 4x − 3","y = 4x + 3","y = −3x + 4","y = 4x"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using slope-intercept form y = mx + b with m = 4 and y-intercept b = −3 (from the point (0, −3)): y = 4x − 3.$q$),
  ($q$alg-44$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A store sells pens for $2 and notebooks for $5. Maria bought a total of 8 items for $25. How many notebooks did she buy?$q$, $q$["3","5","2","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let p + n = 8 and 2p + 5n = 25. Substituting p = 8 − n: 2(8 − n) + 5n = 25 → 16 + 3n = 25 → n = 3.$q$),
  ($q$alg-45$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 3, null, $q$For which value of x is the inequality 5 − 2x ≥ 3x − 10 NOT satisfied?$q$, $q$["4","3","0","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solve the inequality: 5 − 2x ≥ 3x − 10 → 15 ≥ 5x → x ≤ 3. Since 4 > 3, x = 4 does not satisfy the inequality, while the other values do.$q$),
  ($q$alg-46$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$Which ordered pair is a solution to the inequality y > x + 1?$q$, $q$["(2, 5)","(2, 3)","(0, −1)","(3, 2)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For (2, 5): 5 > 2 + 1 = 3, which is true. The other points fail the inequality when substituted.$q$),
  ($q$alg-47$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A tank initially contains 15 gallons of water and is being filled at a rate of 4 gallons per minute. How many minutes will it take for the tank to contain 75 gallons?$q$, $q$["15","20","12","60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$15 + 4m = 75 → 4m = 60 → m = 15.$q$),
  ($q$alg-48$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 3, null, $q$If 3(2x − 1) − 4(x + 2) = 15, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["13","6.5","−13","2.6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3(2x − 1) − 4(x + 2) = 15 → 6x − 3 − 4x − 8 = 15 → 2x − 11 = 15 → 2x = 26 → x = 13.$q$),
  ($q$alg-49$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the y-intercept of the line given by the equation y = −2x + 7?$q$, $q$["7","−2","−7","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In the form y = mx + b, the y-intercept is b = 7.$q$),
  ($q$alg-50$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line passes through (−2, 3) and (2, 11). What is the equation of the line?$q$, $q$["y = 2x + 7","y = 2x + 3","y = 4x + 7","y = 2x − 7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (11 − 3)/(2 − (−2)) = 8/4 = 2. Using point (−2, 3): y − 3 = 2(x + 2) → y = 2x + 7.$q$),
  ($q$alg-51$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 3, null, $q$For what value of c does the system 3x + 4y = 12 and 6x + 8y = c have infinitely many solutions?$q$, $q$["24","12","6","48"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying the first equation by 2 gives 6x + 8y = 24. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, the second equation must be identical, so c = 24.$q$),
  ($q$alg-52$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest integer value of x that satisfies 2x + 3 ≥ 9?$q$, $q$["3","4","2","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2x + 3 ≥ 9 → 2x ≥ 6 → x ≥ 3, so the smallest integer value is 3.$q$),
  ($q$alg-53$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If x − 2y ≥ 8 and x = 0, what is the maximum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["−4","4","−8","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 0: −2y ≥ 8 → y ≤ −4 (the inequality flips when dividing by a negative number), so the maximum value of y is −4.$q$),
  ($q$alg-54$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 3, null, $q$A jar contains only nickels and dimes worth $3.75 in total. There are 45 coins altogether. How many dimes are in the jar?$q$, $q$["30","15","20","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let n + d = 45 and 0.05n + 0.10d = 3.75. Substituting n = 45 − d: 0.05(45 − d) + 0.10d = 3.75 → 2.25 + 0.05d = 3.75 → d = 30.$q$),
  ($q$alg-55$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 7x − 2 = 5x + 12, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["7","14","5","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$7x − 2 = 5x + 12 → 2x = 14 → x = 7.$q$),
  ($q$alg-56$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If 3x + 4y = 24 and x = 4, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["3","4","6","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 4: 3(4) + 4y = 24 → 12 + 4y = 24 → 4y = 12 → y = 3.$q$),
  ($q$alg-57$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function h is linear. If h(2) = 3 and h(−1) = 12, what is the value of h(0)?$q$, $q$["9","3","6","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (12 − 3)/(−1 − 2) = 9/(−3) = −3, so h(x) = −3x + b. Using h(2) = 3: 3 = −6 + b → b = 9. Then h(0) = 9.$q$),
  ($q$alg-58$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x = 3y and 2x + y = 21, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["3","9","7","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 3y into 2x + y = 21: 2(3y) + y = 21 → 7y = 21 → y = 3.$q$),
  ($q$alg-59$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A student needs an average of at least 90 on three tests to earn an A. The student scored 85 and 92 on the first two tests. What is the minimum score needed on the third test to earn an A?$q$, $q$["93","90","88","95"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(85 + 92 + x)/3 ≥ 90 → 177 + x ≥ 270 → x ≥ 93, so the minimum score needed is 93.$q$),
  ($q$geo-1$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 1, null, $q$Two angles of a triangle measure 55° and 65°. What is the measure of the third angle?$q$, $q$["60°","70°","55°","65°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The angles of a triangle sum to 180°: 180 − 55 − 65 = 60°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-2$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 1, null, $q$A triangle with sides 3, 4, and 5 is similar to a second triangle whose shortest side is 9. What is the length of the second triangle's longest side?$q$, $q$["15","12","20","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The scale factor is 9/3 = 3, so the longest side is 5 × 3 = 15.$q$),
  ($q$geo-3$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 1, null, $q$A triangle has a base of 10 and a height of 6. What is its area?$q$, $q$["30","60","16","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Area = (1/2)(base)(height) = (1/2)(10)(6) = 30.$q$),
  ($q$geo-4$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 1, null, $q$A circle has a radius of 5. What is its area?$q$, $q$["25π","10π","5π","50π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Area = πr² = π(5²) = 25π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-5$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, null, $q$A circle has a radius of 12. What is the length of an arc with a central angle of 60°?$q$, $q$["4π","2π","6π","12π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Arc length = (60/360)(2π)(12) = (1/6)(24π) = 4π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-6$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 1, null, $q$A rectangular prism has dimensions 4, 5, and 6. What is its volume?$q$, $q$["120","60","90","100"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Volume = 4 × 5 × 6 = 120.$q$),
  ($q$geo-7$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 2, null, $q$In a right triangle, the side opposite angle θ has length 8, and the hypotenuse has length 17. What is sin(θ)?$q$, $q$["8/17","15/17","8/15","15/8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$sin(θ) = opposite/hypotenuse = 8/17.$q$),
  ($q$geo-8$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 1, null, $q$A right triangle has legs of length 9 and 12. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["15","21","13","225"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$√(9² + 12²) = √(81 + 144) = √225 = 15.$q$),
  ($q$geo-9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 2, null, $q$A cylinder has a radius of 3 and a height of 10. What is its volume?$q$, $q$["90π","30π","60π","9π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Volume = πr²h = π(3²)(10) = 90π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One angle formed measures 70°. What is the measure of its same-side (co-interior) angle?$q$, $q$["110°","70°","90°","20°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Co-interior angles are supplementary: 180 − 70 = 110°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 2, null, $q$If sin(θ) = 3/5 for an acute angle θ, what is cos(θ)?$q$, $q$["4/5","3/4","5/3","5/4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This is a 3-4-5 right triangle: with opposite = 3 and hypotenuse = 5, the adjacent side is 4, so cos(θ) = 4/5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 1, null, $q$A circle is given by the equation (x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 16. What is its radius?$q$, $q$["4","16","2","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The equation is in the form (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r², so r² = 16 and r = 4.$q$),
  ($q$geo-13$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, null, $q$A circle has a radius of 6. What is the area of a sector with a central angle of 90°?$q$, $q$["9π","36π","18π","3π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sector area = (90/360)(π)(6²) = (1/4)(36π) = 9π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-14$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 2, null, $q$In a 30-60-90 triangle, the shorter leg has length 5. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["10","5√3","5√2","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a 30-60-90 triangle, the hypotenuse is twice the shorter leg: 2 × 5 = 10.$q$),
  ($q$geo-15$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 1, null, $q$In a 45-45-90 triangle, each leg has length 7. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["7√2","14","7√3","49"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a 45-45-90 triangle, the hypotenuse equals leg × √2, so the hypotenuse is 7√2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-16$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 2, null, $q$A triangle's exterior angle at one vertex equals the sum of the two remote interior angles, which measure 50° and 65°. What is the measure of the exterior angle?$q$, $q$["115°","65°","50°","180°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Exterior angle = 50 + 65 = 115°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-17$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 1, null, $q$A circle has a circumference of 18π. What is its diameter?$q$, $q$["18","9","36","6π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Circumference = πd → 18π = πd → d = 18.$q$),
  ($q$geo-18$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 2, null, $q$A cone has a radius of 3 and a height of 8. What is its volume?$q$, $q$["24π","72π","216π","8π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Volume = (1/3)πr²h = (1/3)π(9)(8) = 24π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-19$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 2, null, $q$A 13-foot ladder leans against a wall with its base 5 feet from the wall. How high up the wall does the ladder reach?$q$, $q$["12","14","10","144"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$√(13² − 5²) = √(169 − 25) = √144 = 12 feet.$q$),
  ($q$geo-20$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 2, null, $q$A tree casts a 24-foot shadow at the same time a 6-foot person casts an 8-foot shadow. How tall is the tree?$q$, $q$["18","32","20","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using similar triangles: 6/8 = h/24 → h = 6 × (24/8) = 18 feet.$q$),
  ($q$geo-21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 1, null, $q$A cube has edge length 5. What is its surface area?$q$, $q$["150","125","100","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Surface area = 6(edge²) = 6(25) = 150.$q$),
  ($q$geo-22$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$If sin(x°) = cos(30°) and x is an acute angle, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["60","30","45","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since sin(x°) = cos(90° − x°), setting 90 − x = 30 gives x = 60.$q$),
  ($q$geo-23$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, null, $q$An inscribed angle intercepts an arc measuring 100°. What is the measure of the inscribed angle?$q$, $q$["50°","100°","25°","200°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An inscribed angle equals half the measure of its intercepted arc: 100/2 = 50°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-24$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the distance between the points (1, 2) and (4, 6)?$q$, $q$["5","7","25","√13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Distance = √[(4−1)² + (6−2)²] = √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-25$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A cube has a volume of 27. If each side length is doubled, what is the volume of the new cube?$q$, $q$["216","54","108","729"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The original edge length is 3 (since 3³ = 27). Doubling gives an edge of 6, so the new volume is 6³ = 216.$q$),
  ($q$geo-26$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane has equation x² + y² − 6x + 8y = 0. What is the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["5","25","3","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Completing the square: (x² − 6x + 9) + (y² + 8y + 16) = 9 + 16 → (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = 25, so r² = 25 and r = 5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-27$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In a right triangle, one angle measures 30° and the side opposite that angle has length 5. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["10","5√3","15","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$sin(30°) = opposite/hypotenuse = 5/hyp, and sin(30°) = 0.5, so hyp = 5/0.5 = 10.$q$),
  ($q$geo-28$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF, with a scale factor of 3/2 from DEF to ABC. If the area of triangle DEF is 16, what is the area of triangle ABC?$q$, $q$["36","24","32","48"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Area scales by the square of the linear scale factor: (3/2)² = 9/4. So the area of ABC is 16 × 9/4 = 36.$q$),
  ($q$geo-29$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$The diagonal of a square has length 8√2. What is the area of the square?$q$, $q$["64","32","128","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a square with side s, the diagonal equals s√2. So s√2 = 8√2 → s = 8, and the area is s² = 64.$q$),
  ($q$geo-30$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 2, null, $q$In triangle ABC, angle A measures 50° and angle B measures 70°. What is the measure of the exterior angle at vertex C?$q$, $q$["120°","60°","110°","130°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interior angle at C is 180° − 50° − 70° = 60°, so the exterior angle is 180° − 60° = 120° (equivalently, the sum of the two remote interior angles, 50° + 70°).$q$),
  ($q$geo-31$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One angle formed measures 65°. What is the measure of its corresponding angle?$q$, $q$["65°","115°","25°","155°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Corresponding angles formed by a transversal crossing parallel lines are always equal.$q$),
  ($q$geo-32$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$A ladder 13 feet long leans against a wall, with its base 5 feet from the wall. How many feet up the wall does the ladder reach?$q$, $q$["12","11","10","13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$By the Pythagorean theorem: height = √(13² − 5²) = √(169 − 25) = √144 = 12 (a 5-12-13 right triangle).$q$),
  ($q$geo-33$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the midpoint of the segment with endpoints (−2, 5) and (6, −1)?$q$, $q$["(2, 2)","(4, 4)","(2, 3)","(−2, 2)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Midpoint = ((−2 + 6)/2, (5 + (−1))/2) = (4/2, 4/2) = (2, 2).$q$),
  ($q$geo-34$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 1, null, $q$A rectangle has a length of 12 and a width of 7. What is its area?$q$, $q$["84","19","42","98"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Area = length × width = 12 × 7 = 84.$q$),
  ($q$geo-35$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 1, null, $q$A circle has a diameter of 22. What is its circumference?$q$, $q$["22π","11π","44π","121π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Circumference = πd = π(22) = 22π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-36$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line passing through the points (2, 3) and (6, 11)?$q$, $q$["2","1/2","8","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (11 − 3)/(6 − 2) = 8/4 = 2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-37$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 1, null, $q$Two angles are supplementary. One angle measures 112°. What is the measure of the other angle?$q$, $q$["68°","112°","78°","22°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Supplementary angles sum to 180°: 180 − 112 = 68°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-38$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 1, null, $q$In a right triangle, the side adjacent to angle θ has length 7, and the hypotenuse has length 25. What is cos(θ)?$q$, $q$["7/25","24/25","7/24","24/7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$cos(θ) = adjacent/hypotenuse = 7/25 (this is a 7-24-25 right triangle).$q$),
  ($q$geo-39$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 1, null, $q$A right triangle has legs of length 8 and 15. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["17","23","19","289"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$√(8² + 15²) = √(64 + 225) = √289 = 17.$q$),
  ($q$geo-40$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 1, null, $q$Triangle PQR is similar to triangle XYZ, with a scale factor of 4 from PQR to XYZ. If side PQ has length 6, what is the length of the corresponding side XY?$q$, $q$["24","1.5","10","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Corresponding sides scale by the same factor: XY = 6 × 4 = 24.$q$),
  ($q$geo-41$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 1, null, $q$In a 30-60-90 triangle, the hypotenuse has length 14. What is the length of the shorter leg?$q$, $q$["7","7√3","14√3","7√2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a 30-60-90 triangle, the hypotenuse is twice the shorter leg, so the shorter leg is 14/2 = 7.$q$),
  ($q$geo-42$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 1, null, $q$In a triangle, one angle measures 90° and another measures 35°. What is the measure of the third angle?$q$, $q$["55°","45°","65°","125°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The angles of a triangle sum to 180°: 180 − 90 − 35 = 55°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-43$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 2, null, $q$A sphere has a radius of 6. What is its volume, in terms of π?$q$, $q$["288π","216π","576π","96π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Volume = (4/3)πr³ = (4/3)π(6³) = (4/3)π(216) = 288π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-44$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, null, $q$A circle has a radius of 9. What is the area of a sector with a central angle of 40°?$q$, $q$["9π","81π","40π","4.5π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sector area = (40/360)(π)(9²) = (1/9)(81π) = 9π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-45$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 2, null, $q$A line segment has endpoints (−3, 4) and (5, −2). What is the length of the segment?$q$, $q$["10","8","14","100"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Length = √[(5 − (−3))² + (−2 − 4)²] = √(8² + (−6)²) = √(64 + 36) = √100 = 10.$q$),
  ($q$geo-46$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One angle formed measures 48°. What is the measure of its alternate interior angle?$q$, $q$["48°","132°","42°","96°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Alternate interior angles formed by a transversal crossing parallel lines are always equal, so the alternate interior angle also measures 48°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-47$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 2, null, $q$If tan(θ) = 5/12 for an acute angle θ, what is sin(θ)?$q$, $q$["5/13","12/13","5/12","12/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$tan(θ) = 5/12 corresponds to a 5-12-13 right triangle, so with opposite = 5 and hypotenuse = 13, sin(θ) = 5/13.$q$),
  ($q$geo-48$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 2, null, $q$A right triangle has a hypotenuse of length 26 and one leg of length 10. What is the length of the other leg?$q$, $q$["24","16","36","576"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$√(26² − 10²) = √(676 − 100) = √576 = 24.$q$),
  ($q$geo-49$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 2, null, $q$A flagpole casts a 15-foot shadow at the same time a 4-foot mailbox casts a 3-foot shadow. How tall, in feet, is the flagpole?$q$, $q$["20","11.25","18.75","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using similar triangles: 4/3 = h/15 → h = 4 × (15/3) = 20 feet.$q$),
  ($q$geo-50$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 2, null, $q$In a 45-45-90 triangle, the hypotenuse has length 10√2. What is the length of each leg?$q$, $q$["10","10√2","5√2","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a 45-45-90 triangle, leg = hypotenuse/√2, so each leg is (10√2)/√2 = 10.$q$),
  ($q$geo-51$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 2, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle A is twice the measure of angle B, and angle C measures 30°. What is the measure of angle A?$q$, $q$["100°","50°","80°","130°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With A = 2B and A + B + C = 180°: 2B + B + 30 = 180 → 3B = 150 → B = 50°, so A = 2(50) = 100°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-52$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 2, null, $q$A triangle has angle measures in the ratio 2:3:4. What is the measure of the largest angle?$q$, $q$["80°","60°","90°","100°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio parts sum to 2 + 3 + 4 = 9, and 180°/9 = 20° per part, so the largest angle is 4 × 20° = 80°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-53$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A rectangular prism has a square base with side length 4 and a volume of 128. What is the height of the prism?$q$, $q$["8","32","4","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The base area is 4² = 16, so the height is 128/16 = 8.$q$),
  ($q$geo-54$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane has equation x² + y² + 4x − 10y + 13 = 0. What is the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["4","16","5","13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Completing the square: (x² + 4x + 4) + (y² − 10y + 25) = −13 + 4 + 25 → (x + 2)² + (y − 5)² = 16, so r² = 16 and r = 4.$q$),
  ($q$geo-55$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$Line l passes through the point (1, 5) and is perpendicular to a line with slope −2/3. What is the equation of line l in slope-intercept form?$q$, $q$["y = (3/2)x + 7/2","y = (−2/3)x + 17/3","y = (3/2)x − 7/2","y = −(2/3)x + 5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A line perpendicular to slope −2/3 has slope 3/2. Using point (1, 5): y − 5 = (3/2)(x − 1) → y = (3/2)x − 3/2 + 5 = (3/2)x + 7/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-56$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$Two lines intersect, forming four angles. One angle measures (3x + 10)°, and its vertical angle measures (5x − 20)°. What is the value of x?$q$, $q$["15","5","10","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Vertical angles are equal: 3x + 10 = 5x − 20 → 30 = 2x → x = 15.$q$),
  ($q$geo-57$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In a right triangle, angle A measures 40° and the hypotenuse has length 20. What is the length of the side opposite angle A, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["12.9","15.3","16.8","10.2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Opposite = hypotenuse × sin(A) = 20 × sin(40°) ≈ 20 × 0.643 ≈ 12.9.$q$),
  ($q$geo-58$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$A rectangular garden has a diagonal path of length 39 feet. If the garden's width is 15 feet, what is its length, in feet?$q$, $q$["36","34","24","54"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$By the Pythagorean theorem: length = √(39² − 15²) = √(1521 − 225) = √1296 = 36 feet.$q$),
  ($q$geo-59$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$Triangle DEF is similar to triangle GHI, with a scale factor of 5/3 from GHI to DEF. If the perimeter of triangle GHI is 36, what is the perimeter of triangle DEF?$q$, $q$["60","100","21.6","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Unlike area, perimeter scales by the same factor as corresponding sides (not its square): 36 × (5/3) = 60.$q$),
  ($q$geo-60$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a 30-60-90 triangle, the longer leg has length 9√3. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["18","9√3","9√6","27"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The longer leg equals the shorter leg times √3, so the shorter leg is 9√3/√3 = 9. The hypotenuse is twice the shorter leg: 2 × 9 = 18.$q$),
  ($q$geo-61$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the exterior angle at vertex A measures 130°, and angle B measures 55°. What is the measure of angle C?$q$, $q$["75°","65°","70°","85°"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An exterior angle equals the sum of the two remote interior angles: 130 = B + C = 55 + C, so C = 75°.$q$),
  ($q$pd-1$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A car travels 270 miles using 9 gallons of gas. What is the car's fuel efficiency, in miles per gallon?$q$, $q$["30","27","9","270"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$270 miles ÷ 9 gallons = 30 miles per gallon.$q$),
  ($q$pd-2$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$In a class of 32 students, the ratio of boys to girls is 3:5. How many girls are in the class?$q$, $q$["20","12","15","17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio has 8 total parts. Girls = (5/8) · 32 = 20.$q$),
  ($q$pd-3$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A stock rose in value from $40 to $52. What was the percent increase?$q$, $q$["30%","12%","23%","33%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Percent increase = (52 − 40)/40 × 100 = 12/40 × 100 = 30%.$q$),
  ($q$pd-4$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$What is 20% of 60% of 500?$q$, $q$["60","100","120","50"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$60% of 500 = 300. 20% of 300 = 60.$q$),
  ($q$pd-5$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A data set is 3, 5, 5, 7, 9, 50. Which measure of center is most affected by the value 50?$q$, $q$["The mean","The median","The mode","Neither the mean nor the median"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The mean is sensitive to extreme values (outliers), while the median, based on position, changes little when one extreme value is present.$q$),
  ($q$pd-6$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$Which of the following data sets has the greatest standard deviation?$q$, $q$["{1, 10, 10, 19}","{10, 10, 10, 10}","{5, 10, 10, 15}","{9, 10, 10, 11}"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Standard deviation measures spread from the mean. {1, 10, 10, 19} has the widest spread of values around its mean, giving it the greatest standard deviation.$q$),
  ($q$pd-7$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot of study hours versus test score has a line of best fit given by y = 5x + 60, where x is hours studied and y is the test score. What does the slope of this line represent?$q$, $q$["Each additional hour of study is associated with an increase of 5 points on the test.","A student who studies 0 hours is predicted to score 5 points.","Each additional point on the test requires 5 more hours of study.","The maximum possible test score is 5."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a linear model y = mx + b, the slope m represents the change in y per unit increase in x — here, 5 additional points per additional hour studied.$q$),
  ($q$pd-8$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 4 red, 3 blue, and 5 green marbles. What is the probability of randomly drawing a blue marble?$q$, $q$["1/4","1/3","3/5","1/12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 12 total marbles, and 3 are blue, so the probability is 3/12 = 1/4.$q$),
  ($q$pd-9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$Of 200 students, 120 play sports; of those, 80 also play an instrument. Of the 80 students who don't play sports, 50 play an instrument. What is the probability that a randomly selected student who plays an instrument also plays sports?$q$, $q$["8/13","80/200","80/120","50/130"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total instrument players = 80 + 50 = 130. Of those, 80 also play sports, so the probability is 80/130 = 8/13.$q$),
  ($q$pd-10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A poll of 400 randomly selected voters found that 220 favor a proposal. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the percentage of all voters who favor the proposal?$q$, $q$["55%","220%","400%","45%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$220/400 = 0.55 = 55%.$q$),
  ($q$pd-11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$Which change would most directly decrease the margin of error of a survey estimate?$q$, $q$["Increasing the sample size","Decreasing the sample size","Asking a more biased question","Surveying fewer people from the population"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Margin of error generally decreases as sample size increases, because larger samples produce more precise estimates of the population.$q$),
  ($q$pd-12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A student scored 80 on a test worth 30% of the final grade and 90 on a test worth 70% of the final grade. What is the student's weighted average?$q$, $q$["87","85","88","86"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$0.30(80) + 0.70(90) = 24 + 63 = 87.$q$),
  ($q$pd-13$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A printer prints 45 pages in 3 minutes at a constant rate. How many pages will it print in 20 minutes?$q$, $q$["300","45","225","60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rate = 45/3 = 15 pages per minute. In 20 minutes: 15 × 20 = 300 pages.$q$),
  ($q$pd-14$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A recipe that serves 4 people requires 250 grams of flour. How many grams of flour are needed to serve 10 people at the same ratio?$q$, $q$["625","600","1000","400"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$250 × (10/4) = 250 × 2.5 = 625 grams.$q$),
  ($q$pd-15$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A study concludes that 'most people exercise regularly' based on a survey of visitors to a gym. What is the main flaw in this conclusion?$q$, $q$["The sample is not representative of the general population.","The sample size is too large to be useful.","Gym visitors cannot be surveyed accurately.","Exercise habits cannot be measured through surveys."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Surveying only gym visitors overrepresents people who already exercise, so the sample cannot be generalized to the population as a whole.$q$),
  ($q$pd-16$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 3, null, $q$A jar contains 5 red and 7 blue candies. Two candies are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?$q$, $q$["5/33","25/144","5/12","4/11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(both red) = (5/12) × (4/11) = 20/132 = 5/33.$q$),
  ($q$pd-17$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$After a 20% discount, a shirt costs $32. What was the shirt's original price?$q$, $q$["$40","$38.40","$44","$36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If the original price is p, then 0.80p = 32 → p = 40.$q$),
  ($q$pd-18$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$In a scatterplot with a line of best fit, a data point lies far above the line. What does this indicate about that data point?$q$, $q$["Its actual value is greater than the value predicted by the line.","Its actual value is less than the value predicted by the line.","It lies exactly on the line of best fit.","It cannot be used to evaluate the model."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A point above the line of best fit has an actual y-value greater than the model's predicted value at that x.$q$),
  ($q$pd-19$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$An alloy is 40% copper by mass. How many kilograms of copper are in 25 kilograms of the alloy?$q$, $q$["10","15","4","40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$0.40 × 25 = 10 kilograms.$q$),
  ($q$pd-20$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A fair coin is flipped 3 times. What is the probability of getting at least one heads?$q$, $q$["7/8","1/8","3/8","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(at least one heads) = 1 − P(no heads) = 1 − (1/2)³ = 1 − 1/8 = 7/8.$q$),
  ($q$pd-21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A data set has a mean of 70 and a standard deviation of 5. What value is 2 standard deviations above the mean?$q$, $q$["80","75","72","85"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$70 + 2(5) = 70 + 10 = 80.$q$),
  ($q$pd-22$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$In a survey, 60 of 150 respondents preferred Option A. What percent of respondents preferred a different option?$q$, $q$["60%","40%","90%","30%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$150 − 60 = 90 respondents preferred another option. 90/150 = 0.60 = 60%.$q$),
  ($q$pd-23$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A distribution of household incomes is strongly right-skewed, with a small number of very high incomes. Which is most likely true of the mean and median?$q$, $q$["The mean is greater than the median.","The median is greater than the mean.","The mean and median are equal.","The relationship cannot be determined."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a right-skewed distribution, a few very high values pull the mean upward more than they affect the median, so the mean exceeds the median.$q$),
  ($q$pd-24$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A car travels at 90 kilometers per hour. What is this speed in meters per second?$q$, $q$["25","15","30","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$90 km/h = 90,000 m / 3,600 s = 25 m/s.$q$),
  ($q$pd-25$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A company wants to estimate the average commute time for all of its employees. Which sampling method would produce the most reliable estimate?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting 200 employees from the full employee roster","Asking only employees who arrive earliest each day","Surveying employees who volunteer to respond","Asking only employees in the company's headquarters office"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire employee roster is representative of the whole population, unlike the biased methods in the other choices.$q$),
  ($q$pd-26$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot shows a linear relationship between hours studied (x) and exam score (y), modeled by y = 55 + 4x. Based on this model, how many additional hours of studying are needed to raise a predicted score from 75 to 91?$q$, $q$["4","5","9","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving 75 = 55 + 4x gives x = 5; solving 91 = 55 + 4x gives x = 9. The additional hours needed is 9 − 5 = 4.$q$),
  ($q$pd-27$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$A survey of 200 students found that 120 play a sport and 80 play an instrument, with 30 students doing both. What is the probability that a randomly selected student who plays a sport also plays an instrument?$q$, $q$["1/4","3/20","1/6","2/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This is a conditional probability: P(instrument | sport) = (students who do both) / (students who play a sport) = 30/120 = 1/4.$q$),
  ($q$pd-28$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A data set has a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 4. Which value is exactly 2 standard deviations above the mean?$q$, $q$["58","54","46","62"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2 standard deviations above the mean is 50 + 2(4) = 58.$q$),
  ($q$pd-29$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$A factory produces widgets at a constant rate. If it produces 450 widgets in 6 hours, how many widgets does it produce in 1 minute?$q$, $q$["1.25","0.75","1.5","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rate is 450/6 = 75 widgets per hour, and 75/60 = 1.25 widgets per minute.$q$),
  ($q$pd-30$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A map has a scale of 1 inch to 15 miles. If two cities are 4.5 inches apart on the map, how many miles apart are they in reality?$q$, $q$["67.5","60","75","54"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4.5 inches × 15 miles per inch = 67.5 miles.$q$),
  ($q$pd-31$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A random sample of 50 voters from a city found that 60% support a proposed measure, with a margin of error of 5 percentage points. Which is the most reasonable conclusion?$q$, $q$["Between about 55% and 65% of all city voters likely support the measure.","Exactly 60% of all city voters support the measure.","Fewer than half of all city voters support the measure.","The sample is too small to draw any conclusion."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A margin of error defines a plausible range around the sample estimate: 60% ± 5 percentage points gives an interval of about 55% to 65% for the full population.$q$),
  ($q$pd-32$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A store increases the price of an item by 20% and then applies a 20% discount to the new price. Compared to the original price, the final price is:$q$, $q$["4% less than the original price","The same as the original price","4% more than the original price","20% less than the original price"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying by 1.20 then 0.80 gives an overall factor of 0.96, which is 4% less than the original price.$q$),
  ($q$pd-33$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A line of best fit for a data set is given by y = 3x + 2. If the actual y-value for x = 5 is 20, what is the residual?$q$, $q$["3","−3","17","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The predicted value is 3(5) + 2 = 17. The residual is actual minus predicted: 20 − 17 = 3.$q$),
  ($q$pd-34$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A school has a ratio of teachers to students of 2:35. If the school has 70 teachers, how many students does it have?$q$, $q$["1225","1190","1260","700"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio 2:35 scales by a factor of 70/2 = 35. So students = 35 × 35 = 1225.$q$),
  ($q$pd-35$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A hose fills a 60-gallon tank in 15 minutes at a constant rate. How many gallons does it fill in 4 minutes?$q$, $q$["16","15","4","240"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rate is 60/15 = 4 gallons per minute. In 4 minutes: 4 × 4 = 16 gallons.$q$),
  ($q$pd-36$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A laptop originally priced at $800 is on sale for 25% off. What is the sale price?$q$, $q$["$600","$700","$575","$625"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$25% off means paying 75% of the price: 0.75 × 800 = $600.$q$),
  ($q$pd-37$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The data set 12, 15, 15, 18, 20 has a mean of 16. What is the sum of the deviations of each value from the mean?$q$, $q$["0","16","5","80"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sum of deviations from the mean is always 0, since the mean is the balancing point of the data: (−4) + (−1) + (−1) + 2 + 4 = 0.$q$),
  ($q$pd-38$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot of weekly hours worked (x) versus weekly pay (y, in dollars) has a line of best fit given by y = 15x + 20. According to this model, what is the predicted weekly pay for someone who works 30 hours?$q$, $q$["$470","$450","$500","$420"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 30 into y = 15x + 20: y = 15(30) + 20 = 450 + 20 = 470, so the predicted weekly pay is $470.$q$),
  ($q$pd-39$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A standard six-sided die is rolled once. What is the probability of rolling a number greater than 4?$q$, $q$["1/3","1/2","2/3","1/6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rolling a 5 or 6 satisfies the condition, giving 2 favorable outcomes out of 6: 2/6 = 1/3.$q$),
  ($q$pd-40$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A group of 50 people includes 30 who own a car. Of the car owners, 18 also own a bike. What is the probability that a randomly selected car owner also owns a bike?$q$, $q$["3/5","18/50","3/10","5/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(bike | car) = (car owners who also own a bike) / (total car owners) = 18/30 = 3/5.$q$),
  ($q$pd-41$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 250 shoppers found that 100 prefer online shopping over in-store shopping. Based on this sample, what percentage of all shoppers would be expected to prefer online shopping?$q$, $q$["40%","25%","60%","10%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$100/250 = 0.40 = 40%, so the sample suggests about 40% of all shoppers prefer online shopping.$q$),
  ($q$pd-42$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A researcher wants to estimate the average height of adult men in a country. Which method would introduce the least bias?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting adult men from across the entire country","Measuring only men on a professional basketball team","Measuring only men who visit a particular gym","Measuring only men in one small town"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire population avoids the bias introduced by selecting from a narrow or unrepresentative subgroup.$q$),
  ($q$pd-43$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A paint mixture uses blue and yellow paint in a ratio of 5:3 to make green paint. If a batch uses 9 liters of yellow paint, how many total liters of green paint does the batch make?$q$, $q$["24","20","15","27"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Yellow is 3 parts and equals 9 liters, so each part is 3 liters. Blue is 5 parts = 15 liters. Total = 15 + 9 = 24 liters.$q$),
  ($q$pd-44$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A cyclist travels 5 kilometers in 20 minutes at a constant speed. At this rate, how many kilometers will the cyclist travel in 1.5 hours?$q$, $q$["22.5","15","18.75","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rate is 5/20 = 0.25 kilometers per minute. In 90 minutes (1.5 hours): 0.25 × 90 = 22.5 kilometers.$q$),
  ($q$pd-45$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A company's revenue increased by 15% in the first year and then decreased by 10% in the second year. What was the overall percent change in revenue over the two years?$q$, $q$["3.5% increase","5% increase","5% decrease","2.5% increase"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying by 1.15 and then 0.90 gives 1.15 × 0.90 = 1.035, an overall increase of 3.5%.$q$),
  ($q$pd-46$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$Two data sets have the same mean, but data set A has a standard deviation of 2 and data set B has a standard deviation of 8. Which statement is best supported?$q$, $q$["The values in data set B are more spread out from the mean than the values in data set A.","The values in data set A are more spread out from the mean than the values in data set B.","Data sets A and B have identical distributions.","Data set B has a smaller range than data set A."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A higher standard deviation indicates greater spread of values around the mean, so data set B's values are more spread out than data set A's.$q$),
  ($q$pd-47$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows a strong negative linear association between the age of a car (x, in years) and its resale value (y, in dollars), modeled by y = 18000 − 1200x. Based on this model, what is the predicted resale value of a car that is 6 years old?$q$, $q$["$10800","$12000","$9600","$7200"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 6: y = 18000 − 1200(6) = 18000 − 7200 = $10800.$q$),
  ($q$pd-48$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A spinner is divided into 8 equal sections numbered 1 through 8. What is the probability that a single spin lands on an even number or a number greater than 6?$q$, $q$["5/8","3/4","1/2","7/8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Even numbers: {2, 4, 6, 8}. Numbers greater than 6: {7, 8}. Combined without double-counting 8: {2, 4, 6, 7, 8}, which is 5 outcomes out of 8, so the probability is 5/8.$q$),
  ($q$pd-49$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 300 customers found that 180 bought a beverage. Of those who bought a beverage, 45 also bought a snack. Of the 120 customers who did not buy a beverage, 30 bought a snack. What is the probability that a randomly selected customer who bought a snack also bought a beverage?$q$, $q$["3/5","45/180","45/300","30/75"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total snack buyers = 45 + 30 = 75. Of those, 45 also bought a beverage, so the probability is 45/75 = 3/5.$q$),
  ($q$pd-50$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 500 residents found that 340 support building a new park, with a margin of error of 4 percentage points. Which of the following is the most reasonable conclusion about all residents?$q$, $q$["Between about 64% and 72% of all residents likely support the new park.","Exactly 68% of all residents support the new park.","At least 72% of all residents support the new park.","Fewer than 64% of all residents support the new park."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample estimate is 340/500 = 68%. With a margin of error of 4 percentage points, the plausible range for the population is about 64% to 72%.$q$),
  ($q$pd-51$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A news article claims that 'eating chocolate causes people to live longer,' based on an observational study that found chocolate eaters had longer average lifespans than non-eaters. What is the main flaw in this claim?$q$, $q$["The observational study shows correlation, not that chocolate causes longer life, since other factors may explain the link.","Observational studies can never be used to detect any association between variables.","The lifespans of chocolate eaters and non-eaters cannot be compared at all.","The claim would only be flawed if the sample size were smaller than 100."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An observational study can show an association, but without random assignment it cannot establish that chocolate causes longer life; other factors (confounding variables) could explain the pattern.$q$),
  ($q$pd-52$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$A recipe calls for flour, sugar, and butter in the ratio 5:2:1 by weight. If a baker uses 12 ounces of sugar, how many total ounces of the three ingredients combined does the baker use?$q$, $q$["48","40","36","60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sugar is 2 parts and equals 12 ounces, so each part is 6 ounces. Flour = 5 × 6 = 30 ounces, butter = 1 × 6 = 6 ounces. Total = 30 + 12 + 6 = 48 ounces.$q$),
  ($q$pd-53$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$Machine A fills 240 bottles per hour, and Machine B fills 360 bottles per hour. If both machines run together at their constant rates, how many minutes will it take to fill 1000 bottles?$q$, $q$["100","60","120","83.3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Combined rate = 240 + 360 = 600 bottles per hour = 10 bottles per minute. Time to fill 1000 bottles = 1000/10 = 100 minutes.$q$),
  ($q$pd-54$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store marks up the wholesale cost of an item by 40% to set the retail price. During a sale, the retail price is discounted by 30%. If the sale price is $98, what was the original wholesale cost?$q$, $q$["$100","$98","$140","$70"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Retail price = 1.40c. Sale price = 0.70 × 1.40c = 0.98c. Setting 0.98c = 98 gives c = 100.$q$),
  ($q$pd-55$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A data set of 9 values has a mean of 40. When a 10th value is added, the new mean becomes 42. What is the value of the 10th data point?$q$, $q$["60","42","48","50"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The original sum is 9 × 40 = 360. The new sum with 10 values is 10 × 42 = 420. The 10th value is 420 − 360 = 60.$q$),
  ($q$pd-56$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A line of best fit is given by y = 4x + 38. For a particular data point at x = 12, the residual is −5. What is the actual y-value of that data point?$q$, $q$["81","91","86","76"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The predicted value at x = 12 is 4(12) + 38 = 86. Since residual = actual − predicted, the actual value is 86 + (−5) = 81.$q$),
  ($q$pd-57$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 3, null, $q$A box contains 4 red, 6 blue, and 2 green pens. Two pens are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that the first pen is red and the second pen is blue?$q$, $q$["2/11","1/11","4/11","24/144"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(red then blue) = (4/12) × (6/11) = 24/132 = 2/11.$q$),
  ($q$pd-58$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$A company survey of 500 employees found that 300 work remotely at least part-time. Of the employees who work remotely, 180 have more than 5 years of experience. Of the 200 employees who do not work remotely, 60 have more than 5 years of experience. What is the probability that a randomly selected employee with more than 5 years of experience works remotely?$q$, $q$["3/4","180/300","180/500","60/240"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total employees with more than 5 years of experience = 180 + 60 = 240. Of those, 180 work remotely, so the probability is 180/240 = 3/4.$q$),
  ($q$pd-59$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A random sample of 800 adults found that 456 support a new transportation policy, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Which statement is most consistent with this result?$q$, $q$["It is plausible that between 54% and 60% of all adults support the policy.","Exactly 57% of all adults support the policy.","The true population percentage must be exactly 54% or 60%.","The sample size is too small to estimate a population percentage."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample percentage is 456/800 = 57%. With a margin of error of 3 percentage points, the plausible range for the population is 57% − 3% = 54% to 57% + 3% = 60%.$q$),
  ($q$conv-1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Once the votes were tallied______the results were announced to a stunned crowd.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Once the votes were tallied' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The city's water tower had stood for over a century______no one wanted to see it demolished.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," so, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The experiment's outcome confirmed the hypothesis______heat does accelerate the reaction.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the hypothesis was, following a complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Employees______who complete the training will receive a certificate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Who complete the training' is a restrictive (essential) clause identifying which employees, so no comma is needed before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The recipe, ______ was passed down through four generations, calls for a pinch of saffron.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","that","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause is nonessential (set off by commas) and refers to a thing, the recipe, so 'which' is correct; 'that' is reserved for restrictive clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Ticket sales exceeded expectations______therefore, the band added a second show.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A conjunctive adverb like 'therefore' joining two independent clauses requires a semicolon before it (and a comma after).$q$),
  ($q$conv-7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Exhausted from the overnight flight______the team went straight to bed instead of sightseeing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Exhausted from the overnight flight' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The rain continued for hours______the streets began to flood.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with no conjunction must be separated by a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The museum acquired three new pieces this year______a bronze sculpture, an oil painting, and a hand-woven tapestry.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items following the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$In the years following the merger______the company's revenue nearly tripled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'In the years following the merger' is an introductory phrase and requires a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Critics were divided on the film's pacing______audiences overwhelmingly loved it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with a contrast in meaning but no coordinating conjunction must be joined with a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The award, ______ given annually since 1962, honors excellence in local journalism.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which has been","that has been","who has been","being"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nonessential clause set off by commas refers to a thing (the award) and needs a complete verb phrase, so 'which has been' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The bakery sold out of croissants by nine a.m.______the line had formed before sunrise.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," because "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent and closely related, so a semicolon correctly joins them without a coordinating conjunction.$q$),
  ($q$conv-14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The flock of geese______south every autumn.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["migrates","migrate","have migrated","are migrating"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Flock' is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular verb 'migrates.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Neither the coach nor the players______satisfied with the referee's call.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In an 'either/or' or 'neither/nor' construction, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'players,' which is plural, so 'were' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Each of the applicants______required to submit three letters of recommendation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Each' is singular, regardless of the plural noun in the prepositional phrase that follows it, so the singular verb 'is' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$By the time the guests arrive, she______dinner for two hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will have been cooking","cooks","cooked","is cooking"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'By the time' with a future event signals the future perfect progressive tense, 'will have been cooking,' to show an action continuing up to that future point.$q$),
  ($q$conv-18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The data______surprising, prompting the team to repeat the experiment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In formal Standard English, 'data' is treated as a plural noun (the plural of 'datum'), so it takes the plural verb 'were.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The company reminded its employees to submit______expense reports by Friday.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The antecedent 'employees' is plural, so the plural possessive pronoun 'their' is required for agreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The twins each brought______own laptop to the study session.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'The twins' is the plural antecedent, so 'their' is the only pronoun among the choices that agrees with it correctly.$q$),
  ($q$conv-21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Walking through the old district, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["I finally saw the cathedral's spires come into view.","the cathedral's spires finally came into view.","the old district's history was learned by me.","cathedral spires were seen by tourists walking through the district."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Walking through the old district' describes a person, not the spires or the history, so the main clause's subject must be the walker: 'I.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The internship taught her to manage budgets, delegate tasks, and______client meetings.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["lead","leading","how to lead","she led"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence lists parallel infinitive actions ('to manage,' '[to] delegate,' '[to] lead'), so 'lead' maintains the parallel structure.$q$),
  ($q$conv-23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The award recognizes work that is original, rigorous, and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["clearly written","clarity in writing","written with clarity","the writing is clear"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list requires parallel adjective phrases ('original, rigorous, and ___'), and 'clearly written' matches that adjectival form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Yesterday, the technician inspected the wiring and______that a fuse had blown.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["determined","determines","will determine","determining"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence is in past tense ('Yesterday...inspected'), so the second verb must also be past tense, 'determined,' to remain consistent.$q$),
  ($q$conv-25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Of the two finalists, the second speaker was the______prepared.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["more","most","much","many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When comparing exactly two things, the comparative form 'more' is required rather than the superlative 'most,' which is used for three or more.$q$),
  ($q$conv-26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The storm knocked out power across the county______residents relied on generators for nearly a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," "," and, "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses ('The storm knocked out power...' and 'residents relied on generators...') must be joined with a semicolon (or a comma plus conjunction); only the semicolon is offered correctly here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The committee, along with its three advisors,______meeting every Thursday afternoon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["holds","hold","holding","have held"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Along with its three advisors' is a parenthetical phrase, not part of the subject. The true subject is the singular 'committee,' which requires the singular verb 'holds.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Having rehearsed the presentation a dozen times______the intern still felt nervous walking into the boardroom.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," and "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An introductory participial phrase ('Having rehearsed...times') must be separated from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The city council recommended that the new ordinance______before the next fiscal year begins.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["take effect","takes effect","will take effect","taking effect"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Recommended that' triggers the subjunctive mood, which uses the base form of the verb ('take effect') regardless of the subject's number.$q$),
  ($q$conv-30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Only the applicants______submitted every required document were invited to interview.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","who, ",", who",", who,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause 'who submitted every required document' is essential (restrictive) to identifying which applicants are meant, so it takes no surrounding commas.$q$),
  ($q$conv-31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The workshop teaches participants not only how to write persuasive essays but also______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["how to revise them effectively","effective revision techniques being taught","how effective revision is","revising them is effective"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Not only...but also' requires parallel structure. Since the first part is 'how to write persuasive essays,' the second part must match that form: 'how to revise them effectively.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The recipe calls for three ingredients______flour, sugar, and butter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list that follows a complete independent clause ('The recipe calls for three ingredients').$q$),
  ($q$conv-33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Neither the manager nor the employees______aware of the policy change until the memo arrived.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In 'neither...nor' constructions, the verb agrees with the nearer subject. Since 'employees' (plural) is nearer, the plural verb 'were' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The bridge closed for repairs at midnight______traffic was rerouted through downtown for the remainder of the week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction joining them, so a semicolon is required; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Everyone in the auditorium______asked to silence their phones before the performance began.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","are","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The indefinite pronoun 'everyone' is grammatically singular, so it takes the singular verb 'was.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$After weeks of drought______the first heavy rainfall finally soaked the parched fields.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'After weeks of drought' is an introductory prepositional phrase and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The band members loaded______instruments onto the bus before the sun rose.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The antecedent 'band members' is plural, so the plural possessive pronoun 'their' is required for agreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The chef's pantry contained only the essentials______olive oil, garlic, and sea salt.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items following the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$By the time the fire crew arrived, the flames______the roof.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had already consumed","already consumed","have already consumed","were already consuming"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the roof was consumed before another past event (the crew's arrival), the past perfect 'had already consumed' is required.$q$),
  ($q$conv-40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The final vote______a stunning 7-to-2 decision—reshaped the court's approach to digital privacy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The phrase 'a stunning 7-to-2 decision' is a nonessential interrupter already closed with a dash, so it must be opened with a matching dash rather than a comma, colon, or semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Scanning the horizon for storm clouds, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the sailor decided to postpone the voyage.","the voyage was postponed by the sailor.","storm clouds were the sailor's main concern.","the weather forecast was studied carefully."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Scanning the horizon for storm clouds' describes a person performing the action, so the noun immediately following the comma must be that person: 'the sailor.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The negotiations stalled for weeks______both sides finally agreed to bring in a mediator.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent and lack a coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon rather than a comma, which would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The award goes to______the committee considers most deserving.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whomever","whoever","whom","who"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Within the clause, the pronoun functions as the object of 'considers' ('the committee considers whom'), so the objective case 'whomever' is required even though the whole clause follows the preposition 'to.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Startled by the sudden thunderclap______the toddler began to cry.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Startled by the sudden thunderclap' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Of the three routes into town, the coastal road is the______scenic.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["most","more","much","many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When comparing three or more items, the superlative form 'most' is required rather than the comparative 'more,' which is used for exactly two.$q$),
  ($q$conv-46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The panel featured three experts______Dr. Alavi, a virologist; Ms. Ferreira, an epidemiologist; and Mr. Osei, a public health researcher.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list following the complete independent clause; because the items themselves contain commas, semicolons separate the items, but only a colon can introduce the list as a whole.$q$),
  ($q$conv-47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The corporation announced that______quarterly earnings had exceeded projections.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","it's","their","it is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive pronoun 'its' (no apostrophe) is needed to show the corporation's earnings; 'it's' is a contraction of 'it is' and does not fit here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The council approved the new budget______but several residents voiced concerns at the meeting.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'But' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The new policy is intended both to reduce costs and to______employee satisfaction.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["improve","improving","improvement of","the improvement in"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Both...and' requires parallel structure; since the first element is the infinitive 'to reduce,' the second must match with the base verb 'improve' after 'to.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Books______written before 1900 are displayed in the rare collections room.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Written before 1900' is a restrictive (essential) phrase identifying which books are meant, so no comma is needed before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The old photographs had______in the attic for decades before anyone rediscovered them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["lain","laid","lay","layed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The intransitive verb 'lie' (to rest in a place) takes the past participle 'lain' after 'had'; 'laid' is the past participle of the transitive verb 'lay,' which requires a direct object.$q$),
  ($q$conv-52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The proposal was, however______far too costly for the city's current budget.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'However' interrupts a single independent clause rather than joining two clauses, so it must be enclosed in a pair of commas; since the first comma is already given, a matching comma is needed here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Fifteen minutes______enough time to finish the quiz, according to the instructor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When an amount of time is treated as a single unit rather than individual minutes, it takes a singular verb, so 'is' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The bridge was closed for inspection______consequently, commuters faced long delays for a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Consequently' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Her weekend routine consists of jogging, reading, and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["cooking","to cook","she cooks","cooked meals"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list requires parallel gerunds ('jogging, reading, and ___'), so 'cooking' matches the established form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Because the trail was covered in ice______the rangers closed it to hikers until spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Because the trail was covered in ice' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$One of the boxes in the storage room______missing when the inventory was taken.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","are","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject of the sentence is the singular pronoun 'one,' not the object of the preposition 'boxes,' so the singular verb 'was' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Even though the museum extended its hours for the holidays______attendance remained lower than expected.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": ",". "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Even though the museum extended its hours for the holidays' is a dependent clause; ending it with a period would create a sentence fragment, so it must be joined to the independent clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The negotiations proceeded______than either side had anticipated.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["more smoothly","smoother","more smooth","smoothly more"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The verb 'proceeded' must be modified by an adverb, not an adjective, so 'more smoothly' is correct; 'smoother' and 'more smooth' are adjective forms.$q$),
  ($q$cs-1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The senator's speech was praised for its ______: she distilled a complex policy debate into three clear, memorable points without oversimplifying the stakes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["clarity","ambiguity","length","controversy"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The speech 'distilled a complex debate into three clear, memorable points,' which describes clarity, not ambiguity, length, or controversy.$q$),
  ($q$cs-2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Critics called the debut film ______, noting that its jarring shifts between comedy and tragedy left audiences unsure how to react.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["uneven","polished","predictable","silent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Jarring shifts' that left audiences 'unsure how to react' describes tonal inconsistency, which 'uneven' captures precisely.$q$),
  ($q$cs-3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The old bridge, though visually charming, was closed to traffic after an inspection revealed it to be structurally ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unsound","elegant","modern","popular"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bridge was closed for safety reasons despite being charming, so the inspection must have found it structurally unsafe, or 'unsound.'$q$),
  ($q$cs-4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Far from being ______, the committee's final report sparked a decade of heated public debate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["uncontroversial","lengthy","classified","published"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Far from being ___' signals a contrast with what follows: a report that sparked heated debate is the opposite of uncontroversial.$q$),
  ($q$cs-5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The chef's tasting menu was designed to be ______, guiding diners through flavors from delicate to bold over twelve small courses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["progressive","random","repetitive","brief"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A menu that moves deliberately 'from delicate to bold' across many courses is progressive in structure, not random, repetitive, or brief.$q$),
  ($q$cs-6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Investors grew ______ about the startup's prospects after its third consecutive quarter of missed revenue targets.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["skeptical","enthusiastic","indifferent","confident"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Three consecutive quarters of missed targets would logically make investors doubtful, or skeptical, rather than enthusiastic or confident.$q$),
  ($q$cs-7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The professor's lecture style was famously ______; she wove in personal anecdotes, historical tangents, and pop-culture references until the fifty-minute class felt like ten.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["digressive","monotonous","concise","formal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Weaving in 'tangents' and unrelated references describes a digressive style, the opposite of concise or formal.$q$),
  ($q$cs-8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Rather than resolving the mystery, the novel's final chapter only deepens the ______ surrounding the narrator's true identity.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ambiguity","clarity","resolution","certainty"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A final chapter that fails to resolve a mystery and instead 'deepens' the confusion is increasing ambiguity, not clarity or certainty.$q$),
  ($q$cs-9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The city's new noise ordinance proved remarkably ______, cutting complaints in half within a single month of enforcement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["effective","controversial","expensive","confusing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cutting complaints in half quickly is a mark of success, so 'effective' fits; the other choices aren't supported by the given result.$q$),
  ($q$cs-10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Though the two economists ______ on almost every policy question, they found unexpected common ground on the issue of infrastructure spending.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disagreed","collaborated","agreed","lectured"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Though...unexpected common ground' signals a contrast: the economists must generally disagree for finding agreement to be notable.$q$),
  ($q$cs-11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The sculpture's ______ form — all sharp angles and exposed steel — stood in deliberate contrast to the soft curves of the surrounding garden.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["angular","organic","fragile","invisible"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Sharp angles and exposed steel' describes an angular form, which the sentence explicitly contrasts with the garden's 'soft curves.'$q$),
  ($q$cs-12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Editors praised the memoir for its ______ honesty, noting the author's willingness to describe her own failures in unflinching detail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unflinching","guarded","selective","comedic"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence itself defines the quality: honesty shown by describing failures 'in unflinching detail,' so 'unflinching' matches directly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The negotiations remained ______ for months, with neither side willing to make the first concession.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["stalled","swift","cordial","public"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Neither side making a concession 'for months' describes negotiations that are stalled, not swift.$q$),
  ($q$cs-14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Unlike her predecessor's cautious approach, the new CEO's strategy was decidedly ______, entering three new markets within her first year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["aggressive","timid","secretive","gradual"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Entering three new markets in one year, in contrast to a 'cautious' predecessor, describes an aggressive strategy, not a timid or gradual one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The ancient manuscript, water-damaged and missing several pages, offers only a ______ account of the kingdom's final years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fragmentary","comprehensive","vivid","official"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A manuscript that is 'water-damaged and missing several pages' would provide an incomplete, or fragmentary, account — not a comprehensive one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Ants are often assumed to navigate purely by scent trails left by other ants. To test this, researchers blocked all scent trails on a foraging path and found that ants still returned to the nest efficiently, apparently relying on a form of visual memory of landmarks.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that challenges the assumption stated in the first sentence.","It restates the assumption from the first sentence in different words.","It introduces an unrelated topic about ant behavior.","It summarizes the passage's conclusion before any evidence is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The experiment described in the second sentence shows ants navigating without scent trails, directly complicating the assumption introduced in the first sentence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Many assume that the invention of the printing press instantly made books cheap and widely available. In fact, for its first several decades, printed books remained luxury items, often more expensive than hand-copied manuscripts made by efficient scriptoria.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To complicate a popular assumption about the printing press's immediate effects","To argue that hand-copied manuscripts were always superior to printed books","To describe the mechanical process of operating a printing press","To praise the individual credited with inventing the printing press"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text opens with a common assumption and then complicates it with a historical detail ('in fact...'), matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A recent essay opens by describing the smell of rain on dry pavement, then pivots to a discussion of the chemical compound petrichor, before closing with a reflection on why humans across cultures find the scent pleasant.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the essay?$q$, $q$["It moves from a sensory description to a scientific explanation to a broader reflection.","It presents two competing scientific theories and evaluates each in turn.","It argues against a widely held belief using historical evidence.","It compares two different chemical compounds found in nature."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay's stated progression — sensory description, then chemistry, then reflection — matches choice A exactly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The report's introduction outlines the rising cost of shipping. The middle section presents three case studies of companies that redesigned their supply chains. The conclusion offers general recommendations based on those cases.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the middle section of the report?$q$, $q$["To provide concrete examples that support the recommendations offered later","To contradict the claims made in the report's introduction","To define technical terms related to shipping logistics","To criticize the companies discussed as case studies"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Case studies presented before the conclusion's recommendations function as supporting evidence for those recommendations, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The author begins by praising the architect's early work, then spends the remainder of the essay detailing the ways his later buildings abandoned the principles that made the early work distinctive.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To trace a shift in the architect's style and suggest it represented a departure from his strengths","To argue that the architect's later work was universally superior to his early work","To provide a technical manual for building design","To defend the architect against all criticism of his career"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay praises the early work, then shows how later work 'abandoned' those strengths — a structure that traces decline, not defense or unqualified praise.$q$),
  ($q$cs-21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A textbook chapter opens with a dramatic account of a shipwreck, then uses the incident throughout the chapter to illustrate principles of ocean currents and buoyancy.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the opening account in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a memorable, concrete example that anchors abstract scientific concepts discussed later.","It proves that shipwrecks are a common occurrence in the ocean.","It argues against the value of studying ocean currents.","It states the chapter's main conclusion before any explanation is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using a vivid narrative to introduce and later illustrate technical concepts is a common structural device, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The op-ed acknowledges the strongest argument against its position before spending three paragraphs systematically responding to each part of that argument.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Addressing a counterargument directly in order to strengthen the author's own position","Ignoring opposing views entirely to avoid weakening the argument","Presenting only anecdotal evidence without any structured argument","Avoiding any clear thesis so that readers can draw their own conclusions"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Explicitly acknowledging and then systematically rebutting the strongest counterargument is a concession-and-rebuttal strategy, described accurately by choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1: Economist A argues that remote work permanently reduced downtown office demand, citing vacancy rates that remain elevated three years after 2020.
